do $polygo_content$
declare
  d constant jsonb := $content${"units":[{"id":"es-b1-u01","language_code":"es","level":"B1","position":1,"title_es":"Life experiences","title_en":"Experiencias de vida","description_es":"Talk about your life experiences with the present perfect (he visto, he hecho, he probado), ask ¿alguna vez has…?, say never with nunca and jamás, and switch to the preterite when you say when something happened.","emoji":"🌍","objective_es":"You can talk about your life experiences, ask someone if they have ever done something, and tell when and how it happened.","grammar":{"title":"The present perfect for experiences · irregular participles · perfect vs preterite","explanation":"The present perfect is formed with the present of haber + a participle: he, has, ha, hemos, han + probado. Regular participles end in -ado for -ar verbs (probar → probado, viajar → viajado) and -ido for -er / -ir verbs (comer → comido, vivir → vivido, conocer → conocido). Some very common verbs are irregular: hacer → hecho, decir → dicho, ver → visto, escribir → escrito, volver → vuelto, poner → puesto, abrir → abierto, romper → roto. The participle never changes (ella ha probado, ellos han probado) and nothing goes between haber and the participle: object pronouns go before (lo he visto), and so do nunca and jamás (nunca he visto…). In Latin America the present perfect is mainly used for life experiences with no specific time: ¿alguna vez has probado el mole? (have you ever…?), nunca he estado en Oaxaca, he estado en Bogotá varias veces, todavía no he visto el mar. As soon as you say when it happened (en 2023, el año pasado, hace dos años, ayer), use the preterite: fui a Oaxaca en 2023. A typical conversation asks with the perfect and tells the details with the preterite: ¿Has estado en Oaxaca? — Sí, fui en 2023 y me encantó. For everyday finished actions Latin Americans also prefer the preterite (hoy comí, ¿ya comiste?), and many people ask ¿alguna vez probaste…? too; in Spain you will hear the perfect much more often (hoy he comido).","examples":[{"target":"¿Alguna vez has probado el mole?","base":"Have you ever tried mole?"},{"target":"Nunca he estado en Oaxaca.","base":"I’ve never been to Oaxaca."},{"target":"He estado en Bogotá varias veces; la primera vez fui en 2023.","base":"I’ve been to Bogotá several times; the first time I went in 2023."}]},"review_status":"draft"},{"id":"es-b1-u02","language_code":"es","level":"B1","position":2,"title_es":"Habits and hobbies","title_en":"Hábitos y pasatiempos","description_es":"Talk about your habits and hobbies: say what you usually do with suelo, how long you have been doing something with llevo + -ando / -iendo, what you are still doing with sigo, and what you stopped, started or did again with dejé de, empecé a and volví a.","emoji":"🎨","objective_es":"You can talk about your habits and hobbies, say how long you have been doing something, and say what you stopped doing, started doing or did again.","grammar":{"title":"Soler · llevar + gerund · seguir + gerund · dejar de, volver a, empezar a","explanation":"Spanish uses verb + infinitive or verb + gerund combinations (periphrases) to talk about habits and changes. Soler + infinitive means “to usually do”: suelo correr por la mañana. It changes o to ue: suelo, sueles, suele, solemos, suelen. Llevar + time + gerund says how long you have been doing something that still continues: llevo dos años estudiando español (I have been studying Spanish for two years). The same idea can be said with the present + desde hace (estudio español desde hace dos años) or hace… que (hace dos años que estudio español): English uses “have been -ing”, but Spanish uses the present. The gerund ends in -ando for -ar verbs (pintar → pintando) and -iendo for -er / -ir verbs (correr → corriendo, vivir → viviendo). Seguir + gerund means “to still be doing”: sigo viviendo en Monterrey (I still live in Monterrey); seguir changes e to i and its yo form is sigo: sigo, sigues, sigue. Three verbs + a preposition + infinitive describe changes: dejar de (to stop): dejé de tomar café; volver a (to do again): volví a nadar; empezar a (to start): empecé a pintar (z changes to c before e). Don’t confuse volver a + infinitive (again) with volver a + a place (to go back to): volví a Oaxaca. Reflexive verbs keep their pronoun: me relajo, te aburres, me inscribí; with an infinitive it is attached: para relajarme, quiero inscribirme.","examples":[{"target":"Suelo correr por la mañana.","base":"I usually run in the morning."},{"target":"Llevo dos años estudiando español.","base":"I’ve been studying Spanish for two years."},{"target":"Dejé de tomar café y volví a correr.","base":"I stopped drinking coffee and started running again."}]},"review_status":"draft"},{"id":"es-b1-u03","language_code":"es","level":"B1","position":3,"title_es":"Anecdotes","title_en":"Anécdotas","description_es":"Tell an anecdote in order: set the scene with the imperfect, tell the events with the preterite, say what had already happened with había + participle, link the story with en ese momento, en cuanto, por fin and al final, and react to other people’s stories.","emoji":"😅","objective_es":"You can tell a short anecdote in the right order, say what had already happened before something else, and react to someone else’s story (¡Qué susto!, ¡No me digas!).","grammar":{"title":"The pluperfect (había + participle) · telling a story · story connectors","explanation":"The pluperfect tells what had already happened before another moment in the past. It is the imperfect of haber + a participle: había, habías, había, habíamos, habían + -ado (-ar verbs: olvidado) or -ido (-er / -ir verbs: salido). The irregular participles are the ones you know from the present perfect: hecho, visto, dicho, escrito, vuelto, puesto, abierto, roto. Ya often goes with it: cuando llegué, el tren ya había salido (it left before I arrived). Compare: cuando llegué, el tren salió (it left at that moment). Había is never separated from the participle, and object pronouns go before había: lo había dejado, me había equivocado. In a story, the imperfect sets the scene (estaba cansada, había mucha gente), the preterite moves the story forward (me dormí, volví) and the pluperfect goes back to something earlier. To order the events use primero, luego, de repente (suddenly), en ese momento (at that moment), en cuanto (as soon as: en cuanto llegué, llamé), por fin (at last, with relief after waiting) and al final (in the end, how things turned out). Several story verbs take a reflexive pronoun: darse cuenta de que (to realize: me di cuenta, se dio cuenta; dar has no accents in the preterite: di, dio), equivocarse (to make a mistake: me equivoqué, with c → qu before e) and reírse (to laugh: me reí, nos reímos; él / ella se rio, written without an accent since 2010, although you will often see rió).","examples":[{"target":"Cuando llegué a la estación, el tren ya había salido.","base":"When I got to the station, the train had already left."},{"target":"Estaba en el metro y, de repente, me di cuenta de que había olvidado el celular.","base":"I was on the subway and suddenly I realized I had forgotten my phone."},{"target":"Al final, todos nos reímos mucho.","base":"In the end, we all laughed a lot."}]},"review_status":"draft"},{"id":"es-b1-u04","language_code":"es","level":"B1","position":4,"title_es":"Plans and goals","title_en":"Planes y metas","description_es":"Talk about your goals and plans: use the future (tendré, podré, saldré, sabré, pondré), say what you will do when something happens with cuando + subjunctive (cuando tenga, cuando termine), and say when with dentro de and algún día.","emoji":"🎯","objective_es":"You can talk about your goals and plans for the future, say what you will do when something happens and say how long from now it will happen.","grammar":{"title":"The future · cuando + subjunctive · dentro de","explanation":"The simple future is formed by adding the same endings to the whole infinitive of -ar, -er and -ir verbs: -é, -ás, -á, -emos, -án (viajaré, comerás, vivirá, estudiaremos, trabajarán). Every form except nosotros has a written accent, and the stress falls on the ending: tra-ba-ja-RÉ. A few frequent verbs change their stem but keep the endings: tener → tendré, poder → podré, salir → saldré, venir → vendré, poner → pondré, saber → sabré, hacer → haré, decir → diré (and hay → habrá). In everyday Latin American Spanish, ir a + infinitive is the usual way to talk about concrete plans (voy a viajar en junio); the simple future is used for predictions, promises and resolutions, and for plans that feel more distant or less certain (algún día tendré una casa en la playa). When cuando refers to the future, the verb after it goes in the present subjunctive, never in the future: cuando tenga dinero, viajaré (not cuando tendré). The present subjunctive swaps the vowel of the ending: -ar verbs take e (terminar → termine, llegar → llegue) and -er / -ir verbs take a, built on the yo form of the present (tengo → tenga, puedo → pueda). For habits, cuando takes the ordinary present: cuando tengo tiempo, leo (whenever I have time, I read). Dentro de + time means “in … from now”: dentro de dos años (in two years); compare hace dos años (two years ago). In Latin America you will also hear en dos años with the same meaning.","examples":[{"target":"Algún día tendré una casa en la playa.","base":"Some day I will have a house at the beach."},{"target":"Cuando tenga dinero, viajaré a Madrid.","base":"When I have money, I will travel to Madrid."},{"target":"Dentro de dos años, viviré en Mérida.","base":"In two years, I will live in Mérida."}]},"review_status":"draft"},{"id":"es-b1-u05","language_code":"es","level":"B1","position":5,"title_es":"Work and studies","title_en":"Trabajo y estudios","description_es":"Meet the present subjunctive: say what you want or hope others will do (quiero que, espero que) and what is important or necessary (es importante que, es necesario que), apply for a job, get through an interview with usted and write a formal email.","emoji":"💼","objective_es":"You can apply for a job or a scholarship, introduce yourself in an interview using usted, say what you want, hope or need others to do with the subjunctive, and write a short formal email.","grammar":{"title":"Present subjunctive · quiero que, espero que, es importante / necesario que","explanation":"Spanish has a verb mood called the subjunctive. At B1 you use it after expressions of wanting, hoping and necessity when the sentence has two different subjects: quiero que trabajes (I want you to work). English uses an infinitive here (I want you to work); Spanish needs que + subjunctive. With only one subject, or with a general rule, keep the infinitive: quiero trabajar (I want to work), es importante llegar a tiempo (it is important to be on time), but es importante que llegues a tiempo (it is important that you are on time). How to form it: take the yo form of the present, drop the -o and switch the vowel. -ar verbs take e: hablo → hable, hables, hable, hablemos, hablen; -er and -ir verbs take a: como → coma, vivo → viva. Because it starts from the yo form, irregular stems carry over: tengo → tenga, hago → haga, puedo → pueda, conozco → conozca. A few verbs must be learned by heart: ser → sea, ir → vaya, saber → sepa, estar → esté, dar → dé (with an accent to tell it apart from de, of). Spelling keeps the sound: llegar → llegue, buscar → busque. The usted form of the subjunctive is the same as the él / ella form, which is why it also appears in formal requests and ads: mande su currículum. In a formal email, start with Estimado señor: or Estimada señora: (a colon, not a comma), state your purpose with le escribo para + infinitive, and close with Atentamente.","examples":[{"target":"Quiero que hables con el cliente hoy.","base":"I want you to talk to the client today."},{"target":"Es necesario que sepa inglés para este puesto.","base":"You need to know English for this position. (formal)"},{"target":"Espero que te den la beca.","base":"I hope they give you the scholarship."}]},"review_status":"draft"},{"id":"es-b1-u06","language_code":"es","level":"B1","position":6,"title_es":"Travel and culture","title_en":"Viajes y cultura","description_es":"Recommend places and give advice with the subjunctive (te recomiendo que, te aconsejo que, es mejor que), talk about conditions with si + present, give commands with pronouns (pruébalo, llévala), sort out a delayed or cancelled flight and behave politely in another culture.","emoji":"🧳","objective_es":"You can recommend places and customs to a visitor, give advice with the subjunctive, deal with a delay or a cancellation at the airport and talk about what is polite or rude in another culture.","grammar":{"title":"Te recomiendo que + subjunctive · si + present · commands with pronouns","explanation":"Recommending and advising work like wanting (U5): when you advise someone else, use que + subjunctive. Te recomiendo que visites el museo (I recommend that you visit the museum), te aconsejo que lleves una chaqueta (I advise you to take a jacket), es mejor que vayas temprano (it is better for you to go early). With usted, change te to le and use the usted form: le recomiendo que espere aquí. A general piece of advice with no specific person can take the infinitive: es mejor ir temprano. For real conditions, si (if, no accent) goes with the present, never with the future or the present subjunctive: si vas a Oaxaca…; the other half can be the present, ir a, the future or a command: si vas a Oaxaca, prueba el mole. The affirmative tú command is usually the same as the él / ella form of the present: prueba, lleva, visita, saluda. Object pronouns are attached to the end, and a written accent keeps the stress where it was: prueba + lo = pruébalo, lleva + la = llévala. Negative tú commands are the subjunctive, with the pronoun before the verb: no regatees, no llegues tarde, no lo pruebes.","examples":[{"target":"Te recomiendo que pruebes el mole.","base":"I recommend that you try the mole."},{"target":"Si vas a Oaxaca, visita el mercado.","base":"If you go to Oaxaca, visit the market."},{"target":"Es mejor que hagas la reserva antes de tu viaje.","base":"It’s better to make the reservation before your trip."}]},"review_status":"draft"},{"id":"es-b1-u07","language_code":"es","level":"B1","position":7,"title_es":"Health and well-being","title_en":"Salud y bienestar","description_es":"Give and ask for health advice with the conditional (yo que tú, sería mejor), talk about diet, exercise, sleep and stress, and react to other people’s news with me alegra que and me preocupa que + subjunctive.","emoji":"🧘","objective_es":"You can give and ask for health advice, talk about stress and healthy habits, say that the more you do something the better (or worse) you feel, and react with emotion to what another person tells you.","grammar":{"title":"Advice with the conditional · me alegra que / me preocupa que + subjunctive · cuanto más…","explanation":"To give advice, Spanish softens it with the conditional. You already know deberías (you should) + infinitive. To put yourself in the other person’s place, say yo que tú (literally “I, than you”: if I were you) or yo en tu lugar (in your place), followed by a verb in the conditional: yo que tú, descansaría. The conditional is easy to form: take the whole infinitive and add -ía, -ías, -ía, -íamos, -ían, the same endings for -ar, -er and -ir verbs (hablaría, comería, dormiría). The í always carries a written accent. Verbs with an irregular future use the same stem (tendría, podría, saldría). Sería mejor (it would be better) is followed by an infinitive: sería mejor caminar. Verbs of emotion such as me alegra que (I’m glad that) and me preocupa que (it worries me that) take the subjunctive when someone else does the action: me alegra que estés mejor, me preocupa que no duermas. If there is no change of person, use the infinitive: me preocupa dormir poco. The subjunctive “swaps” the vowel: -ar verbs take -e (trabajes, fumes) and -er / -ir verbs take -a (duermas, comas). Finally, to say that two things grow together, use cuanto más…, más / mejor / peor…: cuanto más duermo, mejor me siento (the more I sleep, the better I feel). In Mexico you will hear mientras más… with the same meaning. Cada vez más means more and more: tengo cada vez más estrés.","examples":[{"target":"Yo que tú, descansaría un poco más.","base":"If I were you, I’d rest a bit more."},{"target":"Me preocupa que no duermas bien.","base":"It worries me that you don’t sleep well."},{"target":"Cuanto más duermo, mejor me siento.","base":"The more I sleep, the better I feel."}]},"review_status":"draft"},{"id":"es-b1-u08","language_code":"es","level":"B1","position":8,"title_es":"Technology and communication","title_en":"Tecnología y comunicación","description_es":"Describe apps and devices with relative clauses (que, donde, lo que, el que, quien), say what kind of app you are looking for with the subjunctive, ask for technical help and talk about social media.","emoji":"📱","objective_es":"You can describe apps and devices, say what you are looking for, explain a technical problem and understand the solution, and talk about what you post and share on social media.","grammar":{"title":"Relative clauses: que, donde, lo que, el que, quien · busco una app que + subjunctive","explanation":"Relative words join two ideas in one sentence. Que is the most common and works for people and things (that, which, who): la app que uso, los usuarios que conocí. Unlike English, Spanish never drops it: “the app I use” is always la app que uso. Donde (without an accent) refers to a place: la escuela donde estudio; with an accent, ¿dónde? is only for questions. Lo que means “what” in the sense of “the thing that”: lo que necesito es un celular nuevo; lo que pasa es que… (the thing is…) is the usual way to start explaining a problem. El que, la que, los que, las que mean “the one(s) that” and agree with the noun you leave out: ¿Qué foto? — La que publiqué ayer. Quien (no accent) is for people, mostly after a preposition (la profesora con quien hablo) or meaning “whoever” (quien no reciba el enlace…). Finally, the mood of the verb after que depends on whether the thing is known. If it exists and you know it, use the indicative: tengo una app que funciona sin internet. If you are looking for it and don’t know if it exists, use the subjunctive: busco una app que funcione sin internet, necesito una computadora que sea rápida. Object pronouns still go before the conjugated verb: te lo mando, and le + lo becomes se lo: se lo mando.","examples":[{"target":"Es la app que uso todos los días.","base":"It’s the app I use every day."},{"target":"Lo que pasa es que mi celular no carga bien.","base":"The thing is, my phone isn’t charging properly."},{"target":"Busco una app que sea gratis.","base":"I’m looking for an app that’s free."}]},"review_status":"draft"},{"id":"es-b1-u09","language_code":"es","level":"B1","position":9,"title_es":"Environment and society","title_en":"Medio ambiente y sociedad","description_es":"Talk about the environment and your community: say what is done with se (se recicla, se venden), use the passive with ser + participle (fue creado por…) and give a nuanced opinion with no creo que and dudo que + subjunctive.","emoji":"♻️","objective_es":"You can talk about environmental and community problems, explain what is done to solve them and give a nuanced opinion, saying what you think, what you don’t think and what you doubt.","grammar":{"title":"Se recicla, se venden · fue creado por · no creo que + subjunctive","explanation":"Spanish often says what is done without saying who does it with se + a verb in the third person. When there is a noun, the verb agrees with it: se recicla el papel (paper is recycled), but se reciclan las botellas (bottles are recycled); se vende fruta, se venden bolsas. This is the “passive se”, and it is the most natural way to translate English passives like “is recycled” or “are sold”. Without a noun, se + a singular verb means “people / you / one” in general: aquí se vive bien (people live well here), ¿cómo se dice…? There is also a passive like the English one, with ser + participle + por (by): el mercado fue creado por los vecinos (the market was created by the neighbors). The participle agrees like an adjective: el bosque fue limpiado, la plaza fue limpiada, los árboles fueron plantados. This passive sounds more formal and is typical of news and written texts; in conversation, people prefer se or an active sentence (los vecinos crearon el mercado). Opinions: creo que is followed by the normal verb (the indicative): creo que es la fábrica. But when you deny or doubt something, Spanish switches to the subjunctive: no creo que sea la fábrica (I don’t think it’s the factory), dudo que la ciudad haga algo (I doubt the city will do anything). Present subjunctive endings swap the vowel: -ar verbs take -e (reciclar → recicle, cambiar → cambie), -er / -ir verbs take -a (hacer → haga, tener → tenga); ser → sea and haber → haya are irregular. Note: the subjunctive after dudo que often translates as “will” in English: dudo que cambie = I doubt it will change.","examples":[{"target":"En mi barrio se recicla el papel y se reciclan las botellas.","base":"In my neighborhood, paper is recycled and bottles are recycled."},{"target":"El mercado fue creado por los vecinos del barrio.","base":"The market was created by the people of the neighborhood."},{"target":"Creo que la fábrica contamina, pero no creo que sea el problema más grande.","base":"I think the factory pollutes, but I don’t think it’s the biggest problem."}]},"review_status":"draft"},{"id":"es-b1-u10","language_code":"es","level":"B1","position":10,"title_es":"News and media","title_en":"Noticias y medios","description_es":"Talk about the news and the media you follow, tell a news story, and report what other people said, asked or asked you to do: dijo que era, explicó que iba a, me preguntó si, me pidió que + subjunctive, según.","emoji":"📰","objective_es":"You can tell a piece of news, report what someone said, asked or asked you to do, and say where you get your news and according to whom something happened.","grammar":{"title":"Reported speech: dijo que era · me preguntó si · me pidió que + subjunctive · según","explanation":"To report what someone said in the past, use dijo que, explicó que or contó que + a sentence. As in English, the verb usually moves one step back in time: “es importante” → explicó que era importante (present → imperfect); “va a llover” → dijo que iba a llover (va a → iba a); “el cliente vendrá” → contó que el cliente vendría (future → conditional, which ends in -ía: vendría, tendría, sería). In everyday Latin American Spanish, if what was said is still true, people often keep the present: me dijo que está enfermo (he is still sick). Reported yes / no questions use si (if, without an accent): “¿Tienes tiempo?” → me preguntó si tenía tiempo. Question words keep their accent: me preguntó dónde vivía, cuándo llegaba. Do not confuse preguntar (to ask a question) with pedir (to ask for something, or to ask someone to do something). After pedir que, Spanish uses the subjunctive: me pide que llame (she asks me to call). After me pidió que, Latin Americans very often keep the present subjunctive when the request is still valid: me pidió que la llame mañana. In books and the news you will also see the imperfect subjunctive, me pidió que la llamara: you only need to recognize it at this level. Según (according to) goes before a noun or a subject pronoun: según el periódico, según ella, según tú. Reported questions are statements, so the voice does not rise at the end.","examples":[{"target":"La jefa explicó que el proyecto era muy importante.","base":"The boss explained that the project was very important."},{"target":"Ana me preguntó si tenía tiempo mañana.","base":"Ana asked me if I had time tomorrow."},{"target":"Según el periódico, el incendio ocurrió anoche.","base":"According to the newspaper, the fire happened last night."}]},"review_status":"draft"},{"id":"es-b1-u11","language_code":"es","level":"B1","position":11,"title_es":"Opinions and relationships","title_en":"Opiniones y relaciones","description_es":"Give your opinion with arguments for and against, contrast points of view, disagree politely, talk about friendship and trust, and imagine unreal situations with si tuviera, si pudiera and si fuera.","emoji":"💬","objective_es":"You can give an opinion with arguments, accept or reject someone else’s politely, give advice with si yo fuera tú and imagine what you would do in a hypothetical situation.","grammar":{"title":"Si + imperfect subjunctive + conditional · por un lado / por otro lado · en cambio","explanation":"To imagine something unreal or unlikely now, Spanish uses si + imperfect subjunctive in one half and the conditional in the other: si tuviera tiempo, iría (if I had time, I would go). The imperfect subjunctive is built from the ellos form of the preterite: drop -ron and add -ra (yo, él / ella, usted), -ras (tú), -ramos (nosotros), -ran (ellos, ustedes): tuvieron → tuviera, pudieron → pudiera, fueron → fuera, estuvieron → estuviera, vivieron → viviera. Fuera serves both ser and ir, because their preterites are the same (fueron); context tells them apart. The nosotros form carries a written accent: tuviéramos. You will also hear and read forms in -se (tuviese, pudiese): they mean the same and are more common in Spain and in writing. The conditional adds -ía, -ías, -ía, -íamos, -ían to the infinitive (viviría, trabajaría, iría, sería); a few verbs use the irregular stem of the future: hacer → haría, tener → tendría, poder → podría, decir → diría. Never put the conditional right after si (si tuviera, not si tendría), and the halves can swap places without a comma: iría si pudiera. Compare with A2: si llueve, vamos is a real, possible condition; si tuviera, iría is imagined. To build an argument, open with por un lado (on the one hand) and continue with por otro lado (on the other hand); en cambio (whereas, by contrast) compares two people or things; estar en contra de means to be against.","examples":[{"target":"Si tuviera más tiempo, iría al gimnasio.","base":"If I had more time, I would go to the gym."},{"target":"Si yo fuera tú, hablaría con ella.","base":"If I were you, I would talk to her."},{"target":"Por un lado, la ciudad tiene muchas ventajas; por otro lado, hay mucho ruido.","base":"On the one hand, the city has many advantages; on the other hand, there is a lot of noise."}]},"review_status":"draft"},{"id":"es-b1-u12","language_code":"es","level":"B1","position":12,"title_es":"Problems and solutions","title_en":"Problemas y soluciones","description_es":"Explain a problem with connectors of cause, consequence and concession, guess what is happening with a lo mejor, quizás and seguramente, make a formal complaint and negotiate a solution with customer service or a neighbor.","emoji":"🛠️","objective_es":"You can explain a problem and its causes, make a formal complaint politely and firmly, ask for a refund or a repair and negotiate a solution.","grammar":{"title":"Ya que · por lo tanto · aunque / a pesar de · a lo mejor, quizás, seguramente","explanation":"Connectors make a problem easy to follow. Cause: ya que (since, because) gives a reason the listener knows or can accept and often opens the sentence (ya que tiene garantía, el técnico va a arreglarla); porque is more neutral and usually comes after the main idea. Consequence: por lo tanto (therefore) is more formal than por eso and typical of emails and complaints; in the middle of a sentence it goes between a semicolon or comma and a comma. Concession: aunque + indicative admits a real fact that does not change the result (aunque es nueva, no funciona); a pesar de (in spite of, despite) is followed by a noun or an infinitive, never a conjugated verb (a pesar de la lluvia, a pesar de tener una reserva), and de + el contracts: a pesar del precio. Aunque + subjunctive (“even if”: aunque llueva) comes at the next level. Probability: a lo mejor (maybe) always takes the indicative (a lo mejor es el vecino); quizás (perhaps, also written quizá) usually takes the subjunctive when it comes before the verb (quizás pueda, quizás sea él), although the indicative is also heard; seguramente looks like “surely” but means probably, most likely, and takes the indicative (seguramente está en la oficina). In a formal complaint, use usted / ustedes and set phrases: Estimados señores: (Dear Sir or Madam), Les escribo para quejarme de… (I am writing to complain about…), Solicito un reembolso (I request a refund), Atentamente (Yours faithfully / Sincerely). Quejarse is reflexive and takes de: me quejo del ruido.","examples":[{"target":"Aunque la lavadora es nueva, no funciona.","base":"Although the washing machine is new, it doesn’t work."},{"target":"A lo mejor el técnico viene mañana.","base":"Maybe the technician is coming tomorrow."},{"target":"La lavadora llegó dañada; por lo tanto, solicito un reembolso.","base":"The washing machine arrived damaged; therefore, I request a refund."}]},"review_status":"draft"}],"words":[{"id":"es-la-experiencia","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u01","text":"la experiencia","translation_es":"experience","accepted_translations":["the experience"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":"🌟","example_en":"Vivir en otro país es una experiencia increíble.","example_es":"Living in another country is an incredible experience.","difficulty":1,"tags":["experiences"]},{"id":"es-por-primera-vez","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u01","text":"por primera vez","translation_es":"for the first time","accepted_translations":[],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"Ayer comí mole por primera vez.","example_es":"Yesterday I ate mole for the first time.","difficulty":1,"tags":["experiences","frequency"]},{"id":"es-varias-veces","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u01","text":"varias veces","translation_es":"several times","accepted_translations":[],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"He estado en Guadalajara varias veces.","example_es":"I’ve been to Guadalajara several times.","difficulty":1,"tags":["experiences","frequency"]},{"id":"es-en-mi-vida","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u01","text":"en mi vida","translation_es":"in my life","accepted_translations":[],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"Nunca he visto un lugar tan bonito en mi vida.","example_es":"I’ve never seen such a beautiful place in my life.","difficulty":1,"tags":["experiences"]},{"id":"es-jamas","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u01","text":"jamás","translation_es":"never (ever)","accepted_translations":["never ever"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"Jamás he estado en Europa.","example_es":"I have never ever been to Europe.","difficulty":1,"tags":["experiences","frequency"]},{"id":"es-he-visto","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u01","text":"he visto","translation_es":"I have seen","accepted_translations":[],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":"👀","example_en":"¿La película? Ya la he visto dos veces.","example_es":"The film? I’ve already seen it twice.","difficulty":1,"tags":["present-perfect"]},{"id":"es-he-hecho","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u01","text":"he hecho","translation_es":"I have done","accepted_translations":["I have made"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"Este año he hecho muchos viajes.","example_es":"This year I have taken a lot of trips.","difficulty":1,"tags":["present-perfect"]},{"id":"es-he-dicho","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u01","text":"he dicho","translation_es":"I have said","accepted_translations":["I have told"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"Ya te lo he dicho: el sábado no puedo.","example_es":"I’ve already told you: I can’t on Saturday.","difficulty":1,"tags":["present-perfect"]},{"id":"es-he-escrito","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u01","text":"he escrito","translation_es":"I have written","accepted_translations":[],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":"✍️","example_en":"He escrito un correo a mi profesora.","example_es":"I have written an email to my teacher.","difficulty":1,"tags":["present-perfect"]},{"id":"es-he-vuelto","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u01","text":"he vuelto","translation_es":"I have come back","accepted_translations":["I have returned","I have been back"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"He vuelto a Oaxaca tres veces.","example_es":"I have been back to Oaxaca three times.","difficulty":1,"tags":["present-perfect"]},{"id":"es-he-probado","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u01","text":"he probado","translation_es":"I have tried","accepted_translations":["I have tasted"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"Nunca he probado los chapulines.","example_es":"I have never tried grasshoppers.","difficulty":1,"tags":["present-perfect","food"]},{"id":"es-he-conocido","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u01","text":"he conocido","translation_es":"I have met","accepted_translations":["I have got to know"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"En este viaje he conocido a gente muy simpática.","example_es":"On this trip I have met some very nice people.","difficulty":1,"tags":["present-perfect","people"]},{"id":"es-inolvidable","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u01","text":"inolvidable","translation_es":"unforgettable","accepted_translations":[],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"Fue un viaje inolvidable.","example_es":"It was an unforgettable trip.","difficulty":1,"tags":["experiences","adjectives"]},{"id":"es-el-idioma","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u01","text":"el idioma","translation_es":"language","accepted_translations":["the language"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":"🗣️","example_en":"El español es un idioma muy bonito.","example_es":"Spanish is a very beautiful language.","difficulty":1,"tags":["languages"]},{"id":"es-el-sueno","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u01","text":"el sueño","translation_es":"dream","accepted_translations":["the dream"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":"💭","example_en":"Mi sueño es viajar por todo el mundo.","example_es":"My dream is to travel all over the world.","difficulty":1,"tags":["dreams"]},{"id":"es-cumplir-un-sueno","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u01","text":"cumplir un sueño","translation_es":"to make a dream come true","accepted_translations":["to fulfill a dream","to fulfil a dream"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"El año pasado cumplí un sueño: vi el mar por primera vez.","example_es":"Last year I made a dream come true: I saw the sea for the first time.","difficulty":1,"tags":["dreams"]},{"id":"es-me-sorprendio","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u01","text":"me sorprendió","translation_es":"it surprised me","accepted_translations":["I was surprised by"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":"😮","example_en":"Me sorprendió el tráfico de la Ciudad de México.","example_es":"The traffic in Mexico City surprised me.","difficulty":1,"tags":["feelings"]},{"id":"es-el-pasatiempo","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u02","text":"el pasatiempo","translation_es":"hobby","accepted_translations":["pastime","the hobby"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":"🎨","example_en":"¿Cuál es tu pasatiempo? — Me encanta cocinar.","example_es":"What’s your hobby? — I love cooking.","difficulty":1,"tags":["hobbies"]},{"id":"es-suelo","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u02","text":"suelo","translation_es":"I usually","accepted_translations":["I tend to"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"Suelo correr por la mañana.","example_es":"I usually run in the morning.","difficulty":1,"tags":["habits","periphrases"]},{"id":"es-llevo","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u02","text":"llevo","translation_es":"I have been (+ -ing, with time)","accepted_translations":[],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"Llevo dos años estudiando español.","example_es":"I’ve been studying Spanish for two years.","difficulty":1,"tags":["habits","periphrases"]},{"id":"es-sigo","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u02","text":"sigo","translation_es":"I'm still (+ -ing)","accepted_translations":["I keep (+ -ing)"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"Sigo viviendo en Monterrey.","example_es":"I still live in Monterrey.","difficulty":1,"tags":["habits","periphrases"]},{"id":"es-deje-de","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u02","text":"dejé de","translation_es":"I stopped (doing)","accepted_translations":["I quit"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"Dejé de tomar café el año pasado.","example_es":"I stopped drinking coffee last year.","difficulty":1,"tags":["changes","periphrases"]},{"id":"es-volvi-a","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u02","text":"volví a","translation_es":"I … again","accepted_translations":["I did … again"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"Volví a nadar después de cinco años.","example_es":"I started swimming again after five years.","difficulty":1,"tags":["changes","periphrases"]},{"id":"es-empece-a","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u02","text":"empecé a","translation_es":"I started (to)","accepted_translations":["I began (to)"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"Empecé a pintar en enero.","example_es":"I started painting in January.","difficulty":1,"tags":["changes","periphrases"]},{"id":"es-entrenar","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u02","text":"entrenar","translation_es":"to train","accepted_translations":["to work out"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"Me gusta entrenar por la mañana.","example_es":"I like training in the morning.","difficulty":1,"tags":["sport"]},{"id":"es-el-gimnasio","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u02","text":"el gimnasio","translation_es":"gym","accepted_translations":["the gym"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":"🏋️","example_en":"Voy al gimnasio después del trabajo.","example_es":"I go to the gym after work.","difficulty":1,"tags":["sport","places"]},{"id":"es-correr","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u02","text":"correr","translation_es":"to run","accepted_translations":[],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":"🏃","example_en":"Me gusta correr en el parque.","example_es":"I like running in the park.","difficulty":1,"tags":["sport"]},{"id":"es-nadar","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u02","text":"nadar","translation_es":"to swim","accepted_translations":[],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":"🏊","example_en":"Me encanta nadar en el mar.","example_es":"I love swimming in the sea.","difficulty":1,"tags":["sport"]},{"id":"es-pintar","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u02","text":"pintar","translation_es":"to paint","accepted_translations":[],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":"🖌️","example_en":"Los domingos me gusta pintar.","example_es":"On Sundays I like painting.","difficulty":1,"tags":["hobbies"]},{"id":"es-la-costumbre","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u02","text":"la costumbre","translation_es":"habit, custom","accepted_translations":["habit","custom"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"Tengo la costumbre de leer antes de dormir.","example_es":"I have the habit of reading before going to sleep.","difficulty":1,"tags":["habits"]},{"id":"es-aburrirse","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u02","text":"aburrirse","translation_es":"to get bored","accepted_translations":["to be bored"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":"🥱","example_en":"Los niños se aburren en los viajes largos.","example_es":"Children get bored on long trips.","difficulty":1,"tags":["feelings","reflexive"]},{"id":"es-relajarse","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u02","text":"relajarse","translation_es":"to relax","accepted_translations":[],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":"😌","example_en":"Para relajarme, escucho música.","example_es":"To relax, I listen to music.","difficulty":1,"tags":["feelings","reflexive"]},{"id":"es-el-equipo","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u02","text":"el equipo","translation_es":"team","accepted_translations":["the team"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"Juego en un equipo de fútbol.","example_es":"I play in a football team.","difficulty":1,"tags":["sport"]},{"id":"es-inscribirse","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u02","text":"inscribirse","translation_es":"to sign up","accepted_translations":["to enroll","to enrol","to register"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"Quiero inscribirme en una clase de pintura.","example_es":"I want to sign up for a painting class.","difficulty":1,"tags":["hobbies","reflexive"]},{"id":"es-la-anecdota","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u03","text":"la anécdota","translation_es":"anecdote","accepted_translations":["the anecdote"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":"💬","example_en":"Mi abuelo siempre cuenta anécdotas de su pueblo.","example_es":"My grandfather always tells anecdotes about his town.","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","stories"]},{"id":"es-contar","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u03","text":"contar","translation_es":"to tell","accepted_translations":["to count"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"¿Me cuentas qué pasó?","example_es":"Will you tell me what happened?","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","stories","verbs"]},{"id":"es-la-historia","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u03","text":"la historia","translation_es":"story","accepted_translations":["the story","history"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":"📖","example_en":"Es una historia muy divertida.","example_es":"It’s a very funny story.","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","stories"]},{"id":"es-olvidar","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u03","text":"olvidar","translation_es":"to forget","accepted_translations":[],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"No quiero olvidar este día.","example_es":"I don’t want to forget this day.","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","verbs"]},{"id":"es-habia-olvidado","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u03","text":"había olvidado","translation_es":"I had forgotten","accepted_translations":["he had forgotten","she had forgotten"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"Había olvidado las llaves en casa.","example_es":"I had forgotten my keys at home.","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","pluperfect"]},{"id":"es-habia-salido","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u03","text":"había salido","translation_es":"had left","accepted_translations":["had gone out"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"Cuando llegué, el tren ya había salido.","example_es":"When I arrived, the train had already left.","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","pluperfect"]},{"id":"es-darse-cuenta","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u03","text":"darse cuenta","translation_es":"to realize","accepted_translations":["to notice"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"Me di cuenta de que era muy tarde.","example_es":"I realized that it was very late.","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","verbs"]},{"id":"es-en-ese-momento","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u03","text":"en ese momento","translation_es":"at that moment","accepted_translations":[],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"En ese momento, llegó la policía.","example_es":"At that moment, the police arrived.","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","connectors"]},{"id":"es-por-fin","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u03","text":"por fin","translation_es":"finally (at last)","accepted_translations":["at last","finally"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"¡Por fin llegó el autobús!","example_es":"The bus finally arrived!","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","connectors"]},{"id":"es-al-final","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u03","text":"al final","translation_es":"in the end","accepted_translations":[],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"Al final, todo salió bien.","example_es":"In the end, everything turned out fine.","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","connectors"]},{"id":"es-en-cuanto","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u03","text":"en cuanto","translation_es":"as soon as","accepted_translations":[],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"En cuanto llegué a casa, llamé a mi mamá.","example_es":"As soon as I got home, I called my mom.","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","connectors"]},{"id":"es-equivocarse","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u03","text":"equivocarse","translation_es":"to make a mistake","accepted_translations":["to be wrong"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"Perdón, me equivoqué de número.","example_es":"Sorry, I got the wrong number.","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","verbs"]},{"id":"es-el-susto","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u03","text":"el susto","translation_es":"scare, fright","accepted_translations":["scare","fright"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":"😱","example_en":"¡Qué susto! Pensé que había alguien en la casa.","example_es":"What a scare! I thought there was someone in the house.","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","feelings"]},{"id":"es-reirse","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u03","text":"reírse","translation_es":"to laugh","accepted_translations":[],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":"😂","example_en":"Nos reímos mucho con la película.","example_es":"We laughed a lot at the movie.","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","verbs"]},{"id":"es-la-sorpresa","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u03","text":"la sorpresa","translation_es":"surprise","accepted_translations":["the surprise"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":"🎁","example_en":"La fiesta fue una sorpresa para mi papá.","example_es":"The party was a surprise for my dad.","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","feelings"]},{"id":"es-me-paso","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u03","text":"me pasó","translation_es":"it happened to me","accepted_translations":[],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"Esto me pasó el año pasado en Lima.","example_es":"This happened to me last year in Lima.","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","stories"]},{"id":"es-avergonzado","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u03","text":"avergonzado","translation_es":"embarrassed","accepted_translations":[],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":"😳","example_en":"Estaba muy avergonzada porque llegué tarde.","example_es":"I was very embarrassed because I arrived late.","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","feelings"]},{"id":"es-el-objetivo","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u04","text":"el objetivo","translation_es":"goal","accepted_translations":["the goal","objective"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":"🎯","example_en":"Mi objetivo es hablar inglés muy bien.","example_es":"My goal is to speak English very well.","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","goals"]},{"id":"es-la-meta","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u04","text":"la meta","translation_es":"aim, goal","accepted_translations":["aim","the aim","finish line"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":"🏁","example_en":"Mi meta para este año es ahorrar dinero.","example_es":"My aim for this year is to save money.","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","goals"]},{"id":"es-lograr","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u04","text":"lograr","translation_es":"to achieve","accepted_translations":["to manage to"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"Con mucho trabajo, vas a lograr todo.","example_es":"With a lot of work, you’ll achieve everything.","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","goals","verbs"]},{"id":"es-conseguir","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u04",