cle","accepted_translations":["the article"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"Leí un artículo muy bueno sobre la tormenta.","example_es":"I read a very good article about the storm.","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","media"]},{"id":"es-el-periodista","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u10","text":"el periodista","translation_es":"journalist","accepted_translations":["the journalist","reporter"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":"🎤","example_en":"La periodista habló con los testigos del incendio.","example_es":"The journalist talked to the witnesses of the fire.","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","media","jobs"]},{"id":"es-el-titular","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u10","text":"el titular","translation_es":"headline","accepted_translations":["the headline"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"El titular de hoy habla de la tormenta en Veracruz.","example_es":"Today’s headline is about the storm in Veracruz.","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","media"]},{"id":"es-explico-que","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u10","text":"explicó que","translation_es":"explained that","accepted_translations":["he explained that","she explained that"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"La jefa explicó que el proyecto era muy importante.","example_es":"The boss explained that the project was very important.","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","reported"]},{"id":"es-conto-que","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u10","text":"contó que","translation_es":"told (that)","accepted_translations":["said that","told that"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"Mi hermana me contó que tenía un trabajo nuevo.","example_es":"My sister told me that she had a new job.","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","reported"]},{"id":"es-me-pregunto-si","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u10","text":"me preguntó si","translation_es":"asked me if","accepted_translations":["asked me whether","she asked me if"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"Ana me preguntó si tenía tiempo mañana.","example_es":"Ana asked me if I had time tomorrow.","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","reported"]},{"id":"es-me-pidio-que","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u10","text":"me pidió que","translation_es":"asked me to","accepted_translations":["she asked me to","he asked me to"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"Mi mamá me pidió que la llame esta noche.","example_es":"My mom asked me to call her tonight.","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","reported","subjunctive"]},{"id":"es-segun","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u10","text":"según","translation_es":"according to","accepted_translations":[],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"Según el periódico, mañana va a llover.","example_es":"According to the newspaper, it’s going to rain tomorrow.","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","media"]},{"id":"es-ocurrir","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u10","text":"ocurrir","translation_es":"to happen","accepted_translations":["to occur"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"¿Qué ocurrió anoche en la fábrica?","example_es":"What happened at the factory last night?","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","verbs"]},{"id":"es-anunciar","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u10","text":"anunciar","translation_es":"to announce","accepted_translations":[],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"El gobierno anunció que hoy no hay clases.","example_es":"The government announced that there are no classes today.","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","verbs"]},{"id":"es-el-incendio","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u10","text":"el incendio","translation_es":"fire","accepted_translations":["the fire","blaze"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":"🔥","example_en":"Hubo un incendio en una fábrica del centro.","example_es":"There was a fire in a factory downtown.","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","news"]},{"id":"es-la-tormenta","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u10","text":"la tormenta","translation_es":"storm","accepted_translations":["the storm"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":"⛈️","example_en":"Una tormenta muy fuerte llegó anoche a Veracruz.","example_es":"A very strong storm hit Veracruz last night.","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","news","weather"]},{"id":"es-el-gobierno","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u10","text":"el gobierno","translation_es":"government","accepted_translations":["the government"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":"🏛️","example_en":"El gobierno de la ciudad quiere más bicicletas.","example_es":"The city government wants more bicycles.","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","news"]},{"id":"es-el-testigo","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u10","text":"el testigo","translation_es":"witness","accepted_translations":["the witness"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"Una testigo vio el incendio desde su casa.","example_es":"A witness saw the fire from her house.","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","news"]},{"id":"es-informar","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u10","text":"informar","translation_es":"to report","accepted_translations":["to inform"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"Vamos a informar más en las noticias de la noche.","example_es":"We will report more on the evening news.","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","verbs","media"]},{"id":"es-opinar","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u11","text":"opinar","translation_es":"to give an opinion","accepted_translations":["to have an opinion","to express an opinion"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":"🗣️","example_en":"¿Quién quiere opinar primero?","example_es":"Who wants to give their opinion first?","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","opinions"]},{"id":"es-discutir","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u11","text":"discutir","translation_es":"to argue, to discuss","accepted_translations":["to argue","to discuss"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"No me gusta discutir con mi hermano.","example_es":"I don’t like arguing with my brother.","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","opinions"]},{"id":"es-convencer","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u11","text":"convencer","translation_es":"to convince","accepted_translations":["to persuade"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"No puedo convencer a mi papá.","example_es":"I can’t convince my dad.","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","opinions"]},{"id":"es-la-ventaja","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u11","text":"la ventaja","translation_es":"advantage","accepted_translations":["the advantage"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":"👍","example_en":"Una ventaja de la ciudad es el metro.","example_es":"One advantage of the city is the subway.","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","opinions"]},{"id":"es-la-desventaja","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u11","text":"la desventaja","translation_es":"disadvantage","accepted_translations":["the disadvantage","drawback"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":"👎","example_en":"La desventaja es que hay mucho ruido.","example_es":"The disadvantage is that there is a lot of noise.","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","opinions"]},{"id":"es-estar-en-contra","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u11","text":"estar en contra","translation_es":"to be against","accepted_translations":["to oppose"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"Mi abuela está en contra de los celulares en la mesa.","example_es":"My grandmother is against cell phones at the table.","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","opinions"]},{"id":"es-por-un-lado","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u11","text":"por un lado","translation_es":"on the one hand","accepted_translations":[],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"Por un lado, me gusta mi trabajo; por otro lado, el sueldo es bajo.","example_es":"On the one hand, I like my job; on the other hand, the salary is low.","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","connectors"]},{"id":"es-por-otro-lado","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u11","text":"por otro lado","translation_es":"on the other hand","accepted_translations":[],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"Es caro. Por otro lado, es muy bueno.","example_es":"It’s expensive. On the other hand, it’s very good.","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","connectors"]},{"id":"es-en-cambio","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u11","text":"en cambio","translation_es":"however, on the other hand","accepted_translations":["whereas","by contrast"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"Yo prefiero el campo; mi hermana, en cambio, prefiere la ciudad.","example_es":"I prefer the countryside; my sister, on the other hand, prefers the city.","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","connectors"]},{"id":"es-si-tuviera","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u11","text":"si tuviera","translation_es":"if I had","accepted_translations":["if she had","if he had"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"Si tuviera dinero, iría a Madrid.","example_es":"If I had money, I would go to Madrid.","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","conditional"]},{"id":"es-si-pudiera","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u11","text":"si pudiera","translation_es":"if I could","accepted_translations":["if she could","if he could"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"Si pudiera, viviría cerca del mar.","example_es":"If I could, I would live near the sea.","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","conditional"]},{"id":"es-si-fuera","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u11","text":"si fuera","translation_es":"if it were, if I were","accepted_translations":["if I were","if it were","if I was","if it was"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"Si yo fuera tú, no iría.","example_es":"If I were you, I wouldn’t go.","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","conditional"]},{"id":"es-iria","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u11","text":"iría","translation_es":"I would go","accepted_translations":["she would go","he would go"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"Iría contigo, pero tengo que trabajar.","example_es":"I would go with you, but I have to work.","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","conditional"]},{"id":"es-haria","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u11","text":"haría","translation_es":"I would do","accepted_translations":["I would make","she would do","he would do"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"Yo no haría eso.","example_es":"I wouldn’t do that.","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","conditional"]},{"id":"es-la-amistad","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u11","text":"la amistad","translation_es":"friendship","accepted_translations":["the friendship"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":"🤝","example_en":"La amistad es muy importante para mí.","example_es":"Friendship is very important to me.","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","relationships"]},{"id":"es-confiar","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u11","text":"confiar","translation_es":"to trust","accepted_translations":["to rely on"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"Puedes confiar en mí.","example_es":"You can trust me.","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","relationships"]},{"id":"es-el-respeto","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u11","text":"el respeto","translation_es":"respect","accepted_translations":["the respect"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"Con todo respeto, no estoy de acuerdo.","example_es":"With all due respect, I don’t agree.","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","relationships"]},{"id":"es-aunque","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u12","text":"aunque","translation_es":"although","accepted_translations":["even though"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"Aunque estoy cansado, voy a la fiesta.","example_es":"Although I’m tired, I’m going to the party.","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","connectors"]},{"id":"es-por-lo-tanto","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u12","text":"por lo tanto","translation_es":"therefore","accepted_translations":["so","consequently"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"No tengo garantía; por lo tanto, tengo que pagar.","example_es":"I don’t have a warranty; therefore, I have to pay.","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","connectors"]},{"id":"es-ya-que","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u12","text":"ya que","translation_es":"since, because","accepted_translations":["since","as"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"Ya que llueve, nos quedamos en casa.","example_es":"Since it’s raining, we’re staying at home.","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","connectors"]},{"id":"es-a-pesar-de","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u12","text":"a pesar de","translation_es":"in spite of","accepted_translations":["despite"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"A pesar de la lluvia, salimos.","example_es":"In spite of the rain, we went out.","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","connectors"]},{"id":"es-a-lo-mejor","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u12","text":"a lo mejor","translation_es":"maybe","accepted_translations":[],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"A lo mejor llueve mañana.","example_es":"Maybe it will rain tomorrow.","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","probability"]},{"id":"es-quizas","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u12","text":"quizás","translation_es":"perhaps","accepted_translations":[],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"Quizás pueda ir el sábado.","example_es":"Perhaps I can go on Saturday.","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","probability"]},{"id":"es-seguramente","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u12","text":"seguramente","translation_es":"probably, surely","accepted_translations":["probably","most likely"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"Seguramente está en la oficina.","example_es":"She’s probably at the office.","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","probability"]},{"id":"es-la-queja","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u12","text":"la queja","translation_es":"complaint","accepted_translations":["the complaint"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"Mi vecino tiene una queja: hay mucho ruido.","example_es":"My neighbor has a complaint: there is a lot of noise.","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","complaints"]},{"id":"es-quejarse","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u12","text":"quejarse","translation_es":"to complain","accepted_translations":[],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"Mi abuelo siempre se queja del clima.","example_es":"My grandfather always complains about the weather.","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","complaints"]},{"id":"es-reclamar","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u12","text":"reclamar","translation_es":"to claim, to complain","accepted_translations":["to claim","to demand"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"Voy a reclamar mi dinero.","example_es":"I’m going to claim my money back.","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","complaints"]},{"id":"es-el-reembolso","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u12","text":"el reembolso","translation_es":"refund","accepted_translations":["the refund"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":"💸","example_en":"Solicito un reembolso.","example_es":"I request a refund.","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","complaints"]},{"id":"es-la-garantia","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u12","text":"la garantía","translation_es":"warranty","accepted_translations":["guarantee","the warranty"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"La garantía es de un año.","example_es":"The warranty lasts one year.","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","complaints"]},{"id":"es-arreglar","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u12","text":"arreglar","translation_es":"to fix","accepted_translations":["to repair"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":"🔧","example_en":"El técnico va a arreglar la lavadora.","example_es":"The technician is going to fix the washing machine.","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","problems"]},{"id":"es-resolver","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u12","text":"resolver","translation_es":"to solve","accepted_translations":["to resolve","to sort out"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"Quiero resolver el problema.","example_es":"I want to solve the problem.","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","problems"]},{"id":"es-el-tecnico","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u12","text":"el técnico","translation_es":"technician","accepted_translations":["the technician","repairman"],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":"🧑‍🔧","example_en":"El técnico viene el jueves.","example_es":"The technician is coming on Thursday.","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","problems"]},{"id":"es-danado","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u12","text":"dañado","translation_es":"damaged","accepted_translations":[],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"Mi celular está dañado.","example_es":"My cell phone is damaged.","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","problems"]},{"id":"es-el-servicio-al-cliente","language_code":"es","unit_id":"es-b1-u12","text":"el servicio al cliente","translation_es":"customer service","accepted_translations":[],"ipa":null,"romanization":null,"emoji":null,"example_en":"Llamé al servicio al cliente.","example_es":"I called customer service.","difficulty":1,"tags":["b1","complaints"]}],"lessons":[{"id":"es-b1-u01-l01","unit_id":"es-b1-u01","position":1,"title_es":"¿Alguna vez has…?","objective_es":"Ask someone if they have ever done something and answer about your own experiences.","intro":{"explanation":"The present perfect is haber in the present + a participle. Haber goes: (yo) he, (tú) has, (él / ella / usted) ha, (nosotros) hemos, (ellos / ustedes) han. The h is silent, so he sounds like the English “eh”. Regular participles end in -ado (probar → probado, visitar → visitado) or -ido (comer → comido, vivir → vivido). Ver is irregular: visto. Use this tense for life experiences when you don’t say when: ¿Alguna vez has probado…? means “Have you ever tried…?”. Answer Sí, he probado… or No, nunca he probado… (nunca goes before he). En mi vida (in my life) adds emphasis: nunca he visto nada tan bonito en mi vida. Notice the double negative: nunca… nada is correct Spanish.","examples":[{"target":"¿Alguna vez has probado el mole? — Sí, lo he probado en Puebla.","base":"Have you ever tried mole? — Yes, I’ve tried it in Puebla."},{"target":"Nunca he visto el mar.","base":"I’ve never seen the sea."}]},"takeaways":["You form the present perfect with he, has, ha, hemos, han + a participle (probado, vivido, visto).","You ask about experiences with ¿Alguna vez has…? and answer with Sí, he… or No, nunca he…"]},{"id":"es-b1-u01-l02","unit_id":"es-b1-u01","position":2,"title_es":"Hecho, dicho, escrito, vuelto","objective_es":"Use the most common irregular participles to talk about what you have done.","intro":{"explanation":"Some of the most frequent verbs have irregular participles, and it is best to learn them together with he: hacer → he hecho, decir → he dicho, escribir → he escrito, volver → he vuelto, ver → he visto. You will also meet puesto (poner), abierto (abrir) and roto (romper). The participle is the same for every person: yo he hecho, ellos han hecho. Hecho is used both for “done” and “made”: he hecho un viaje (I have taken a trip), he hecho un pastel. Jamás means never, but it is stronger than nunca, a bit like “never ever”: jamás he dicho eso. Like nunca, it can go before the verb or after it with no: no he dicho eso jamás.","examples":[{"target":"Este año he hecho tres viajes.","base":"This year I have taken three trips."},{"target":"Jamás he dicho eso.","base":"I have never said that."}]},"takeaways":["You use the irregular participles hecho, dicho, escrito and vuelto (and visto, abierto, roto).","You say never with nunca or with the stronger jamás."]},{"id":"es-b1-u01-rev1","unit_id":"es-b1-u01","position":3,"title_es":"Review 1","objective_es":"Consolidate “¿Alguna vez has…?” and “Hecho, dicho, escrito, vuelto”.","intro":null,"takeaways":[]},{"id":"es-b1-u01-l03","unit_id":"es-b1-u01","position":4,"title_es":"Ya, todavía no, varias veces","objective_es":"Say how many times you have done something and what you have or haven’t done yet.","intro":{"explanation":"To say how often: una vez (once), dos veces (twice), varias veces (several times), muchas veces, por primera vez (for the first time). Vez is feminine, so it is la primera vez. Ya (already, yet) and todavía no (not yet) often go with the present perfect: ¿Ya has probado la birria? — Todavía no. Conocer means to meet a person or to get to know a place. With a person Spanish adds a: he conocido a mucha gente; with a place, no a: todavía no he conocido Mérida. El idioma (language) is masculine although it ends in -a, like el problema: los idiomas.","examples":[{"target":"¿Ya has probado la birria? — Todavía no.","base":"Have you tried birria yet? — Not yet."},{"target":"He estado en Bogotá varias veces.","base":"I’ve been to Bogotá several times."}]},"takeaways":["You say how many times with una vez, varias veces and por primera vez.","You use ya and todavía no with the present perfect, and conocer a with people."]},{"id":"es-b1-u01-l04","unit_id":"es-b1-u01","position":5,"title_es":"¿Cuándo fue?","objective_es":"Say when an experience happened, switching to the preterite as soon as you give a time.","intro":{"explanation":"The present perfect talks about an experience without a date: he estado en Oaxaca. As soon as you say when (en 2023, el año pasado, hace dos años, en julio), Spanish uses the preterite: fui a Oaxaca en 2023. So a typical conversation asks with the perfect and continues with the preterite: ¿Has estado en Oaxaca? — Sí, fui en 2023 y me encantó. Me sorprendió (it surprised me) works like me gustó: the thing that surprises you is the subject, so with a plural it is me sorprendieron los precios. Inolvidable (unforgettable) is the same for masculine and feminine: un viaje inolvidable, una experiencia inolvidable.","examples":[{"target":"He estado en Oaxaca. Fui en 2023 con mi hermana.","base":"I’ve been to Oaxaca. I went in 2023 with my sister."},{"target":"Me sorprendió el mole: es dulce y picante.","base":"The mole surprised me: it’s sweet and spicy."}]},"takeaways":["You use the present perfect for an experience without a date and the preterite when you say when.","You say what surprised you with me sorprendió and describe an inolvidable experience."]},{"id":"es-b1-u01-rev2","unit_id":"es-b1-u01","position":6,"title_es":"Review 2","objective_es":"Consolidate “Ya, todavía no, varias veces” and “¿Cuándo fue?”.","intro":null,"takeaways":[]},{"id":"es-b1-u01-l05","unit_id":"es-b1-u01","position":7,"title_es":"Cumplir un sueño","objective_es":"Talk about your dreams and the experiences that changed you, combining both past tenses.","intro":{"explanation":"El sueño means dream (it also means sleepiness: tengo sueño, I’m sleepy). Mi sueño es + infinitive tells what you dream of doing: mi sueño es vivir en otro país. Cumplir un sueño is “to make a dream come true”: with a date, use the preterite (el año pasado cumplí un sueño); without one, the perfect (he cumplido un sueño). Now combine everything from this unit: the perfect for your experiences (he estado, he probado, nunca he visto…), and the preterite to tell when and how they happened (fui en 2022, me sorprendió…).","examples":[{"target":"Mi sueño es vivir en otro país.","base":"My dream is to live in another country."},{"target":"El año pasado cumplí un sueño: fui a Japón.","base":"Last year I made a dream come true: I went to Japan."}]},"takeaways":["You talk about dreams with el sueño and cumplir un sueño.","You combine the present perfect (your experiences) and the preterite (when and how) to talk about your life."]},{"id":"es-b1-u01-listen","unit_id":"es-b1-u01","position":8,"title_es":"Listening practice","objective_es":"Recognize the words and phrases of the unit by ear.","intro":null,"takeaways":[]},{"id":"es-b1-u01-words","unit_id":"es-b1-u01","position":9,"title_es":"Words of the unit","objective_es":"Fix the vocabulary of the unit: recognize it and write it.","intro":null,"takeaways":[]},{"id":"es-b1-u01-final","unit_id":"es-b1-u01","position":10,"title_es":"Final review","objective_es":"Show everything from “Life experiences”.","intro":null,"takeaways":[]},{"id":"es-b1-u02-l01","unit_id":"es-b1-u02","position":1,"title_es":"Suelo…","objective_es":"Talk about your habits and hobbies with soler + infinitive.","intro":{"explanation":"Soler + infinitive means “to usually do something”. It changes o to ue: (yo) suelo, (tú) sueles, (él / ella / usted) suele, (nosotros) solemos, (ellos / ustedes) suelen. The next verb is always an infinitive: suelo ir, suelo correr. It means about the same as generalmente or normalmente + present (generalmente voy al gimnasio); both are very common. Careful: el suelo is also a noun (the floor), but in suelo correr it is the verb. El pasatiempo (hobby) comes from pasar + tiempo, “to pass time”. La costumbre is a habit or a custom: tengo la costumbre de leer (I’m in the habit of reading).","examples":[{"target":"Suelo ir al gimnasio por la mañana.","base":"I usually go to the gym in the morning."},{"target":"¿Qué sueles hacer los fines de semana?","base":"What do you usually do at weekends?"}]},"takeaways":["You talk about what you usually do with suelo, sueles, suele + infinitive.","You talk about your hobbies (el pasatiempo) and habits (la costumbre)."]},{"id":"es-b1-u02-l02","unit_id":"es-b1-u02","position":2,"title_es":"Llevo dos años…","objective_es":"Say how long you have been doing something with llevar + time + gerund and desde hace.","intro":{"explanation":"For something that started in the past and is still going on, English uses “have been -ing”, but Spanish uses the present. There are three common ways: llevo dos años nadando, nado desde hace dos años, and hace dos años que nado. Llevar is regular: llevo, llevas, lleva, llevamos, llevan; to ask, say ¿Cuánto tiempo llevas + gerund? The gerund ends in -ando for -ar verbs (entrenar → entrenando, nadar → nadando) and -iendo for -er / -ir verbs (correr → corriendo, vivir → viviendo). For something you haven’t done for a long time, use hace… que no: hace años que no nado. El equipo is a team (it can also mean equipment).","examples":[{"target":"Llevo tres meses entrenando en este gimnasio.","base":"I’ve been training at this gym for three months."},{"target":"Juego en un equipo de fútbol desde hace dos años.","base":"I’ve been playing in a football team for two years."}]},"takeaways":["You say how long you have been doing something with llevo + time + gerund (llevo dos años nadando).","You also use desde hace and hace… que with the present: nado desde hace un año."]},{"id":"es-b1-u02-rev1","unit_id":"es-b1-u02","position":3,"title_es":"Review 1","objective_es":"Consolidate “Suelo…” and “Llevo dos años…”.","intro":null,"takeaways":[]},{"id":"es-b1-u02-l03","unit_id":"es-b1-u02","position":4,"title_es":"Sigo…","objective_es":"Say what you are still doing with seguir + gerund, and talk about relaxing and getting bored.","intro":{"explanation":"Seguir + gerund means “to still be doing something” or “to keep doing it”: sigo viviendo en Monterrey (I still live in Monterrey). Seguir changes e to i and its yo form is sigo: sigo, sigues, sigue, seguimos, siguen. You can say the same with todavía + present: todavía vivo en Monterrey. The opposite is ya no (no longer): ya no pinto. Relajarse (to relax) and aburrirse (to get bored) are reflexive: the pronoun changes with the person (me relajo, te relajas, se relaja; me aburro, te aburres, se aburren). With an infinitive, the pronoun is attached to the end: para relajarme (to relax).","examples":[{"target":"Sigo viviendo en Monterrey.","base":"I still live in Monterrey."},{"target":"Cuando pinto, me relajo y nunca me aburro.","base":"When I paint, I relax and I never get bored."}]},"takeaways":["You say what you are still doing with sigo, sigues, sigue + gerund.","You talk about relaxing and getting bored with relajarse and aburrirse (me relajo, me aburro)."]},{"id":"es-b1-u02-l04","unit_id":"es-b1-u02","position":5,"title_es":"Dejé de…, volví a…","objective_es":"Say what you stopped doing and what you started doing again.","intro":{"explanation":"Dejar de + infinitive means to stop doing something: dejé de tomar café (I stopped drinking coffee). Without de, dejar means to leave or to let. Volver a + infinitive means to do something again: volví a nadar (I swam again, I went back to swimming). Don’t confuse it with volver a + a place, which means to go back: volví a Oaxaca. In the preterite both verbs are regular: dejé, dejaste, dejó; volví, volviste, volvió (the o → ue change is only in the present: vuelvo). To say you don’t do something any more, use ya no + present: ya no corro.","examples":[{"target":"Dejé de correr por una lesión, pero el mes pasado volví a entrenar.","base":"I stopped running because of an injury, but last month I started training again."},{"target":"¿Cuándo dejaste de pintar?","base":"When did you stop painting?"}]},"takeaways":["You say what you stopped doing with dejé de + infinitive.","You say what you did again with volví a + infinitive."]},{"id":"es-b1-u02-rev2","unit_id":"es-b1-u02","position":6,"title_es":"Review 2","objective_es":"Consolidate “Sigo…” and “Dejé de…, volví a…”.","intro":null,"takeaways":[]},{"id":"es-b1-u02-l05","unit_id":"es-b1-u02","position":7,"title_es":"Empecé a…","objective_es":"Tell how you started a new hobby and sign up for an activity.","intro":{"explanation":"Empezar a + infinitive means to start doing something: empecé a pintar en enero. In the yo form of the preterite, z changes to c before e: empecé (but empezaste, empezó). In the present it changes e to ie: empiezo, empiezas. Inscribirse en means to sign up for: me inscribí en un equipo, quiero inscribirme en una clase. Now put the whole unit together: suelo + infinitive for habits, llevo + time + gerund for how long, sigo + gerund for what continues, and dejé de, volví a and empecé a for the changes in your life.","examples":[{"target":"Empecé a pintar en enero y ya no me aburro los domingos.","base":"I started painting in January and I don’t get bored on Sundays any more."},{"target":"Me inscribí en un equipo de vóleibol.","base":"I signed up for a volleyball team."}]},"takeaways":["You say when you started something with empecé a + infinitive.","You sign up for a class or a team with inscribirse en (me inscribí en…)."]},{"id":"es-b1-u02-listen","unit_id":"es-b1-u02","position":8,"title_es":"Listening practice","objective_es":"Recognize the words and phrases of the unit by ear.","intro":null,"takeaways":[]},{"id":"es-b1-u02-words","unit_id":"es-b1-u02","position":9,"title_es":"Words of the unit","objective_es":"Fix the vocabulary of the unit: recognize it and write it.","intro":null,"takeaways":[]},{"id":"es-b1-u02-final","unit_id":"es-b1-u02","position":10,"title_es":"Final review","objective_es":"Show everything from “Habits and hobbies”.","intro":null,"takeaways":[]},{"id":"es-b1-u03-l01","unit_id":"es-b1-u03","position":1,"title_es":"¿Qué te pasó?","objective_es":"Ask someone what happened, start telling a story and react to it.","intro":{"explanation":"An anécdota is a short, real story, usually funny or surprising; la historia is any story (and also history). Contar (to tell) changes o to ue: cuento, cuentas, cuenta, contamos, cuentan. To invite someone to tell you a story say ¿Me cuentas? or cuéntame (tell me), and to start one say te cuento… (let me tell you). Pasar works with me, te, le: ¿Qué te pasó? (What happened to you?), me pasó algo increíble. Remember the two past tenses of a story: the imperfect for the scene (era de noche, llovía) and the preterite for what happened (me pasó, vi). People react with ¡Qué…! + noun: ¡Qué susto! (What a scare!), ¡Qué sorpresa! (What a surprise!), ¡Qué historia!","examples":[{"target":"¿Qué te pasó? — Te cuento: me pasó algo muy divertido.","base":"What happened to you? — Let me tell you: something very funny happened to me."},{"target":"¡Qué susto! ¿Y qué hiciste?","base":"What a scare! And what did you do?"}]},"takeaways":["You ask ¿Qué te pasó? and start a story with te cuento… or me pasó…","You react to a story with ¡Qué susto!, ¡Qué sorpresa! or ¡Qué historia!"]},{"id":"es-b1-u03-l02","unit_id":"es-b1-u03","position":2,"title_es":"Ya había salido","objective_es":"Say what had already happened before another moment in the past.","intro":{"explanation":"To go back to something that happened before another past moment, use the pluperfect: había + participle. Había is the same word as in había mucha gente, and its h is silent: a-BÍ-a. The forms are había, habías, había, habíamos, habían, and the participle never changes: había olvidado, habían salido. The d of -ado / -ido is soft, almost like the th of this. Ya (already) usually goes before había: cuando llegué, el tren ya había salido. Darse cuenta de que means to realize: me di cuenta, te diste cuenta, se dio cuenta. Dar has no written accents in the preterite (di, dio).","examples":[{"target":"Cuando llegué a la estación, el tren ya había salido.","base":"When I got to the station, the train had already left."},{"target":"Me di cuenta de que había olvidado las llaves.","base":"I realized that I had forgotten my keys."}]},"takeaways":["You say what had already happened with había + participle (había salido, había olvidado).","You say what you realized with me di cuenta de que…"]},{"id":"es-b1-u03-rev1","unit_id":"es-b1-u03","position":3,"title_es":"Review 1","objective_es":"Consolidate “¿Qué te pasó?” and “Ya había salido”.","intro":null,"takeaways":[]},{"id":"es-b1-u03-l03","unit_id":"es-b1-u03","position":4,"title_es":"En ese momento","objective_es":"Put the events of a story in order with connectors.","intro":{"explanation":"Connectors make a story easy to follow. Primero, luego and después put events in order; de repente (suddenly) and en ese momento (at that moment) introduce the key event; en cuanto (as soon as) + a past verb says that one thing happened right after another: en cuanto llegué, llamé. Por fin (at last) shows relief after waiting or trying: ¡Por fin llegó el autobús! Al final (in the end) says how things turned out: al final, todo salió bien. With de, al final de means at the end of: al final de la línea, al final de la calle.","examples":[{"target":"En cuanto llegué a casa, llamé a mi mamá.","base":"As soon as I got home, I called my mom."},{"target":"¡Por fin llegó el autobús!","base":"The bus finally arrived!"}]},"takeaways":["You order a story with luego, de repente, en ese momento and en cuanto.","You say how it ended with por fin (at last) and al final (in the end)."]},{"id":"es-b1-u03-l04","unit_id":"es-b1-u03","position":5,"title_es":"Me equivoqué","objective_es":"Talk about mistakes and embarrassing moments, and say who laughed.","intro":{"explanation":"Equivocarse (to make a mistake, to be wrong) and reírse (to laugh) take a reflexive pronoun: me equivoqué, te equivocaste, se equivocó; me reí, te reíste, nos reímos. In the yo form, c becomes qu before e: equivoqué (like busqué). Equivocarse de + noun means to get the wrong one: me equivoqué de número, de autobús, de día. Reírse de means to laugh at or about something. The accent on the í of reí and reímos keeps the two vowels in separate syllables: re-Í-mos. Avergonzado (avergonzada for a woman) means embarrassed. Careful: embarazada means pregnant!","examples":[{"target":"Perdón, me equivoqué de número.","base":"Sorry, I got the wrong number."},{"target":"Estaba muy avergonzada, pero al final nos reímos mucho.","base":"I was very embarrassed, but in the end we laughed a lot."}]},"takeaways":["You say you made a mistake with me equivoqué (de…).","You say how you felt with avergonzado / avergonzada and who laughed with reírse."]},{"id":"es-b1-u03-rev2","unit_id":"es-b1-u03","position":6,"title_es":"Review 2","objective_es":"Consolidate “En ese momento” and “Me equivoqué”.","intro":null,"takeaways":[]},{"id":"es-b1-u03-l05","unit_id":"es-b1-u03","position":7,"title_es":"¡Qué historia!","objective_es":"Tell a complete anecdote and react to someone else’s story.","intro":{"explanation":"A good anecdote has a scene (imperfect: hacía calor, estaba cansada), a series of events (preterite: llegué, me di cuenta), something that had happened before (había olvidado…) and an ending (al final…). When you listen to a story, show interest: ¿Y qué pasó después?, ¿Y entonces?, ¡No me digas! (No way! / You don’t say!), ¡Qué susto!, ¡Qué sorpresa! or ¡Qué vergüenza! (How embarrassing!). Exclamations go up and down with a lot of energy; the follow-up questions rise at the end.","examples":[{"target":"¡No me digas! ¿Y qué pasó después?","base":"No way! And what happened next?"},{"target":"¡Qué vergüenza! Yo también me equivoqué una vez.","base":"How embarrassing! I made a mistake like that once too."}]},"takeaways":["You tell a complete anecdote: scene, events, what had happened before and the ending.","You react to someone’s story with ¡No me digas!, ¡Qué vergüenza! and ¿Y qué pasó después?"]},{"id":"es-b1-u03-listen","unit_id":"es-b1-u03","position":8,"title_es":"Listening practice","objective_es":"Recognize the words and phrases of the unit by ear.","intro":null,"takeaways":[]},{"id":"es-b1-u03-words","unit_id":"es-b1-u03","position":9,"title_es":"Words of the unit","objective_es":"Fix the vocabulary of the unit: recognize it and write it.","intro":null,"takeaways":[]},{"id":"es-b1-u03-final","unit_id":"es-b1-u03","position":10,"title_es":"Final review","objective_es":"Show everything from “Anecdotes”.","intro":null,"takeaways":[]},{"id":"es-b1-u04-l01","unit_id":"es-b1-u04","position":1,"title_es":"Mis metas","objective_es":"Talk about your goals and your plans with ir a.","intro":{"explanation":"El objetivo and la meta both mean goal (la meta is also the finish line of a race); mi meta es + infinitive is a natural way to state one: mi meta es hablar español. Lograr means to achieve (lograr un objetivo, lograr + infinitive). Conseguir means to get or obtain (conseguir un trabajo) and also to manage to do something; it changes e to i like seguir: consigo, consigues, consigue, conseguimos, and in the preterite conseguí, consiguió. Remember that for concrete plans, the most common form in Latin America is ir a + infinitive: voy a estudiar, vamos a viajar.","examples":[{"target":"Mi meta es hablar español muy bien.","base":"My goal is to speak Spanish very well."},{"target":"Voy a conseguir un trabajo nuevo.","base":"I’m going to get a new job."}]},"takeaways":["You talk about your goals with el objetivo, la meta, lograr and conseguir.","You talk about concrete plans with ir a + infinitive."]},{"id":"es-b1-u04-l02","unit_id":"es-b1-u04","position":2,"title_es":"Tendré, podré, saldré","objective_es":"Talk about the future and make resolutions with the simple future.","intro":{"explanation":"The simple future adds -é, -ás, -á, -emos, -án to the whole infinitive: estudiar → estudiaré, aprender → aprenderé, vivir → viviré. Some frequent verbs shorten their stem but keep the same endings: tener → tendré, poder → podré, salir → saldré. Listen for the stress: it falls on the ending, which carries a written accent (ten-DRÉ, po-DRÁS), except in the nosotros form (tendremos). In conversation, ir a is more common for concrete plans; the simple future sounds more like a resolution, a promise or a prediction, and goes well with algún día (some day). New Year’s resolutions are los propósitos de año nuevo: in Mexico and many other countries, people eat twelve grapes at midnight and make a wish for each month of the year.","examples":[{"target":"Algún día tendré una casa en la playa.","base":"Some day I will have a house at the beach."},{"target":"Mañana saldré temprano de la oficina.","base":"Tomorrow I will leave the office early."}]},"takeaways":["You form the future with the infinitive + -é, -ás, -á, -emos, -án (estudiaré).","You use tendré, podré and saldré for resolutions and plans, and algún día for the distant future."]},{"id":"es-b1-u04-rev1","unit_id":"es-b1-u04","position":3,"title_es":"Review 1","objective_es":"Consolidate “Mis metas” and “Tendré, podré, saldré”.","intro":null,"takeaways":[]},{"id":"es-b1-u04-l03","unit_id":"es-b1-u04","position":4,"title_es":"Sabré, pondré · dentro de","objective_es":"Use more irregular futures and say how long from now something will happen.","intro":{"explanation":"More verbs with a short stem in the future: saber → sabré, poner → pondré, hacer → haré, decir → diré, venir → vendré. Saber + infinitive means to know how to: sabré cocinar (I’ll know how to cook). Poner la mesa is to set the table. To say how long from now something will happen, use dentro de + time: dentro de dos semanas (in two weeks). It looks forward, while hace + time looks back: hace dos semanas (two weeks ago). In Latin America you will also hear en dos semanas with the same meaning.","examples":[{"target":"Dentro de una semana sabré si tengo el trabajo.","base":"In a week I will know if I have the job."},{"target":"Te lo diré mañana.","base":"I will tell you tomorrow."}]},"takeaways":["You use sabré, pondré, haré, diré and vendré.","You say how long from now with dentro de + time (dentro de dos años)."]},{"id":"es-b1-u04-l04","unit_id":"es-b1-u04","position":5,"title_es":"Cuando tenga dinero…","objective_es":"Say what you will do when something happens, and talk about saving and moving.","intro":{"explanation":"When cuando talks about the future, the verb after it goes in the present subjunctive, never in the future: cuando tenga dinero, viajaré (not cuando tendré). The subjunctive swaps the vowel of the ending: -ar verbs take e (terminar → termine, llegar → llegue) and -er / -ir verbs take a, starting from the yo form (tengo → tenga). The other half of the sentence uses the future, ir a or the present: cuando termine, te llamo. For habits, cuando takes the ordinary present: cuando tengo tiempo, leo (whenever I have time, I read). Mudarse is to move house: me voy a mudar or voy a mudarme (the pronoun goes before the verb or on the infinitive). Ahorrar is to save money.","examples":[{"target":"Cuando tenga dinero, viajaré a Madrid.","base":"When I have money, I will travel to Madrid."},{"target":"Cuando termine la universidad, voy a mudarme a Lima.","base":"When I finish university, I’m going to move to Lima."}]},"takeaways":["You say what you will do when something happens with cuando + subjunctive (cuando tenga, cuando termine).","You talk about habits with cuando + present (cuando tengo tiempo…)."]},{"id":"es-b1-u04-rev2","unit_id":"es-b1-u04","position":6,"title_es":"Review 2","objective_es":"Consolidate “Sabré, pondré · dentro de” and “Cuando tenga dinero…”.","intro":null,"takeaways":[]},{"id":"es-b1-u04-l05","unit_id":"es-b1-u04","position":7,"title_es":"Dentro de cinco años","objective_es":"Talk about your future in a connected way and react to other people’s plans.","intro":{"explanation":"To talk about your future, combine everything: ir a for concrete plans (voy a estudiar), the future for predictions and resolutions (tendré, podré), cuando + subjunctive for what depends on something else (cuando tenga trabajo…) and dentro de or algún día for when. To sound less certain, use creo que + future (creo que viviré en Lima) or no sé si… (I don’t know if…). To react to someone’s plans: ¡Qué buena idea!, ¡Ojalá que sí! (I hope so!) or ¡Vas a lograrlo! (You’ll make it!).","examples":[{"target":"Creo que dentro de cinco años tendré mi propio negocio.","base":"I think that in five years I will have my own business."},{"target":"Cuando tenga el trabajo, saldremos a cenar.","base":"When I get the job, we will go out for dinner."}]},"takeaways":["You talk about your future combining ir a, the future, cuando + subjunctive and dentro de.","You show doubt with creo que and no sé si, and react with ¡Ojalá que sí!"]},{"id":"es-b1-u04-listen","unit_id":"es-b1-u04","position":8,"title_es":"Listening practice","objective_es":"Recognize the words and phrases of the unit by ear.","intro":null,"takeaways":[]},{"id":"es-b1-u04-words","unit_id":"es-b1-u04","position":9,"title_es":"Words of the unit","objective_es":"Fix the vocabulary of the unit: recognize it and write it.","intro":null,"takeaways":[]},{"id":"es-b1-u04-final","unit_id":"es-b1-u04","position":10,"title_es":"Final review","objective_es":"Show everything from “Plans and goals”.","intro":null,"takeaways":[]},{"id":"es-b1-u05-l01","unit_id":"es-b1-u05","position":1,"title_es":"Quiero que…","objective_es":"Say what you want or hope someone else will do with quiero que and espero que + subjunctive.","intro":{"explanation":"Compare quiero trabajar (I want to work: one subject) with quiero que trabajes (I want you to work: two subjects). When the person who wants and the person who acts are different, Spanish uses que + a new verb form, the present subjunctive. English says I want you to…, Spanish says literally “I want that you work”. To form it, take the yo form of the present, drop the -o and swap the vowel: -ar verbs take e (trabajo → trabaje, trabajes), -er and -ir verbs take a (como → coma, vivo → viva). Irregular yo forms carry over: tengo → tenga, tengas. Espero que works the same way: espero que tengas un buen día. You already know one subjunctive: ojalá que no llueva. A few forms are special: dar → dé, den (espero que me den el puesto).","examples":[{"target":"Mi jefe quiere que llegue a las ocho.","base":"My boss wants me to arrive at eight."},{"target":"Espero que tengas un buen día.","base":"I hope you have a good day."}]},"takeaways":["You say what you want someone else to do with quiero que + subjunctive.","You form the regular subjunctive from the yo form: trabajo → trabaje, tengo → tenga."]},{"id":"es-b1-u05-l02","unit_id":"es-b1-u05","position":2,"title_es":"Es importante que…","objective_es":"Say what is important or necessary for a job with es importante que and es necesario que, and use the most common irregular subjunctives.","intro":{"explanation":"Es importante que and es necesario que are followed by the subjunctive when they talk about a specific person: es importante que llegues a tiempo (that you are on time). For a general rule, use the infinitive: es importante llegar a tiempo. Some very common verbs have irregular subjunctives that you need to learn now: ser → sea, ir → vaya, saber → sepa, estar → esté, hacer → haga, poder → pueda, tener → tenga. Job ads use them a lot, often with usted, whose form is the same as él / ella: es necesario que sepa inglés. Esté has an accent mark (esté, not este, this). El turno is a work shift: el turno de la mañana, de la tarde, de la noche.","examples":[{"target":"Es importante que llegues a tiempo a tu turno.","base":"It’s important that you get to your shift on time."},{"target":"Es necesario que sepa inglés.","base":"You need to know English. (formal)"}]},"takeaways":["You say what is necessary or important for someone with es necesario / importante que + subjunctive.","You recognize the irregular subjunctives sea, vaya, sepa, esté, haga, pueda and tenga."]},{"id":"es-b1-u05-rev1","unit_id":"es-b1-u05","position":3,"title_es":"Review 1","objective_es":"Consolidate “Quiero que…” and “Es importante que…”.","intro":null,"takeaways":[]},{"id":"es-b1-u05-l03","unit_id":"es-b1-u05","position":4,"title_es":"La entrevista","objective_es":"Introduce yourself in a job interview using usted and talk about your degree and your experience.","intro":{"explanation":"In a job interview people use usted, which takes the él / ella forms: ¿Qué carrera estudió? (What did you study?), ¿Por qué quiere trabajar con nosotros? La carrera is your university degree program (four or five years in most of Latin America): estudié la carrera de ingeniería. It can also mean career. Contratar is to hire; when you hire a person, add the personal a: contratar a dos ingenieros. Polite commands to usted use the subjunctive form too: siéntese, por favor (sit down, please).","examples":[{"target":"¿Qué carrera estudió usted?","base":"What did you study? (formal)"},{"target":"La empresa quiere contratar a dos ingenieros.","base":"The company wants to hire two engineers."}]},"takeaways":["You answer interview questions about your degree and experience with usted.","You talk about hiring with contratar a + person."]},{"id":"es-b1-u05-l04","unit_id":"es-b1-u05","position":5,"title_es":"Le escribo para…","objective_es":"Understand and write a formal work email, and talk about deadlines, promotions and resigning.","intro":{"explanation":"A formal email in Spanish opens with Estimado señor: or Estimada señora: (estimado agrees with the person, and a colon follows, not a comma). To a group: Estimados señores: or Estimado equipo:. State why you are writing with le escribo para + infinitive (les escribo to several people, using ustedes). Close with Atentamente (Sincerely) and your name. Renunciar a means to resign from: renunciar a un puesto. Careful with el ascenso (promotion): el ascensor is the elevator.","examples":[{"target":"Estimada señora: Le escribo para solicitar el puesto.","base":"Dear Madam, I am writing to apply for the position."},{"target":"Rodrigo renunció a su puesto la semana pasada.","base":"Rodrigo resigned from his position last week."}]},"takeaways":["You write a formal email with estimado, le escribo para… and atentamente.","You talk about deadlines, promotions and resigning from a job."]},{"id":"es-b1-u05-rev2","unit_id":"es-b1-u05","position":6,"title_es":"Review 2","objective_es":"Consolidate “La entrevista” and “Le escribo para…”.","intro":null,"takeaways":[]},{"id":"es-b1-u05-l05","unit_id":"es-b1-u05","position":7,"title_es":"La beca","objective_es":"Understand a conversation about a scholarship and a job ad, and use the subjunctive to talk about requirements and wishes.","intro":{"explanation":"La beca is a scholarship or grant: solicitar una beca (to apply for a scholarship). Requirements are often given with es necesario que + subjunctive: es necesario que tengas buenas notas (las notas = grades). Wishes and good luck use espero que: ¡espero que te den la beca! When a pronoun goes with an infinitive, it is attached to the end: quiero solicitarla (la = la beca).","examples":[{"target":"Quiero solicitar una beca para estudiar en Madrid.","base":"I want to apply for a scholarship to study in Madrid."},{"target":"Es necesario que tengas buenas notas.","base":"You need to have good grades."}]},"takeaways":["You understand the requirements and the deadline for a scholarship.","You wish someone luck with espero que + subjunctive."]},{"id":"es-b1-u05-listen","unit_id":"es-b1-u05","position":8,"title_es":"Listening practice","objective_es":"Recognize the words and phrases of the unit by ear.","intro":null,"takeaways":[]},{"id":"es-b1-u05-words","unit_id":"es-b1-u05","position":9,"title_es":"Words of the unit","objective_es":"Fix the vocabulary of the unit: recognize it and write it.","intro":null,"takeaways":[]},{"id":"es-b1-u05-final","unit_id":"es-b1-u05","position":10,"title_es":"Final review","objective_es":"Show everything from “Work and studies”.","intro":null,"takeaways":[]},{"id":"es-b1-u06-l01","unit_id":"es-b1-u06","position":1,"title_es":"Te recomiendo que…","objective_es":"Recommend places and give advice to a friend with te recomiendo que and te aconsejo que + subjunctive.","intro":{"explanation":"To recommend something to a friend, use te recomiendo que + subjunctive: te recomiendo que visites el museo (I recommend that you visit the museum). Te aconsejo que (I advise you to) works the same way. The subjunctive is formed as in U5: visito → visite, visites; llevo → lleve, lleves; hago → haga, hagas; ir → vaya, vayas. To someone you call usted, say le recomiendo que + usted form: le recomiendo que visite el museo. Recomendar changes e to ie: recomiendo, recomiendas. El recorrido is a tour or a route through a place, and el destino is your destination.","examples":[{"target":"Te recomiendo que visites el museo de Puebla.","base":"I recommend that you visit the museum in Puebla."},{"target":"Te aconsejo que hagas el recorrido por la mañana.","base":"I advise you to do the tour in the morning."}]},"takeaways":["You recommend places with te recomiendo que + subjunctive.","You give advice with te aconsejo que + subjunctive."]},{"id":"es-b1-u06-l02","unit_id":"es-b1-u06","position":2,"title_es":"Si vas a Oaxaca…","objective_es":"Give travel advice with si + present and es mejor que + subjunctive, and talk about markets, handicrafts and traditions.","intro":{"explanation":"For a real condition, use si (if, with no accent) + present. The other half can be a present, ir a + infinitive, the future or a command: si vas a Oaxaca, prueba el mole; si viajas en noviembre, vas a ver el Día de Muertos. Never put the future or the present subjunctive right after si. The tú command is usually the él / ella form of the present: prueba, visita, lleva. Es mejor que + subjunctive gives softer advice: es mejor que vayas temprano. In many Latin American markets you can regatear (haggle) a little, but never in restaurants or big shops.","examples":[{"target":"Si vas a Oaxaca, prueba el mole.","base":"If you go to Oaxaca, try the mole."},{"target":"Es mejor que vayas temprano al mercado.","base":"It’s better if you go to the market early."}]},"takeaways":["You give travel tips with si + present and a command or ir a.","You give softer advice with es mejor que + subjunctive."]},{"id":"es-b1-u06-rev1","unit_id":"es-b1-u06","position":3,"title_es":"Review 1","objective_es":"Consolidate “Te recomiendo que…” and “Si vas a Oaxaca…”.","intro":null,"takeaways":[]},{"id":"es-b1-u06-l03","unit_id":"es-b1-u06","position":4,"title_es":"Pruébalo, llévala","objective_es":"Give commands with pronouns (pruébalo, llévala), say what not to do (no regatees) and talk about polite and rude behavior.","intro":{"explanation":"With an affirmative tú command, object pronouns are attached to the end of the verb: prueba + lo = pruébalo (try it), lleva + la = llévala (take it). The written accent keeps the stress on the same syllable as before. Lo or la agrees with the thing: el mole → pruébalo, la chaqueta → llévala. Negative tú commands use the subjunctive, and the pronoun goes before the verb: no regatees, no llegues tarde, no lo pruebes. Careful with educado: it means polite, well-mannered, not educated. Grosero is rude.","examples":[{"target":"El mole está muy rico: pruébalo.","base":"The mole is delicious: try it."},{"target":"No regatees en los restaurantes: es grosero.","base":"Don’t haggle in restaurants: it’s rude."}]},"takeaways":["You give commands with attached pronouns: pruébalo, llévala.","You say what not to do with no + subjunctive and talk about polite and rude behavior."]},{"id":"es-b1-u06-l04","unit_id":"es-b1-u06","position":5,"title_es":"Un vuelo con retraso","objective_es":"Deal with a delayed or cancelled flight at the airport and talk about reservations and accommodation, using usted.","intro":{"explanation":"At an airline counter or a hotel desk people use usted, so advice becomes le recomiendo que + usted form: le recomiendo que espere aquí. El retraso is a delay (el vuelo tiene un retraso de dos horas); in South America you will also hear la demora. Cancelar is to cancel. La reserva is a reservation; in Mexico people often say la reservación. El alojamiento is accommodation: a hotel, an apartment, a room. A condition with si works with usted too: si cancelan el vuelo, la aerolínea le paga el hotel.","examples":[{"target":"Mi vuelo tiene un retraso de dos horas.","base":"My flight is two hours late."},{"target":"Si cancelan el vuelo, ¿quién paga el alojamiento?","base":"If they cancel the flight, who pays for the accommodation?"}]},"takeaways":["You explain a delay or a cancellation and ask what happens next.","You understand advice with usted: le recomiendo que espere."]},{"id":"es-b1-u06-rev2","unit_id":"es-b1-u06","position":6,"title_es":"Review 2","objective_es":"Consolidate “Pruébalo, llévala” and “Un vuelo con retraso”.","intro":null,"takeaways":[]},{"id":"es-b1-u06-l05","unit_id":"es-b1-u06","position":7,"title_es":"Otras costumbres","objective_es":"Compare customs between countries, understand advice for a trip and recommend places and good manners to a visitor.","intro":{"explanation":"Customs change from place to place. In Mexico and Peru, people usually greet with one kiss on the cheek between women or between a man and a woman, while men shake hands (se dan la mano). Arriving a little late to a party at someone’s home is normal, but for a work appointment (una cita de trabajo) you should be on time. To give this kind of advice, use te aconsejo que, te recomiendo que or es mejor que + subjunctive. Cusco is more than 3,000 meters high, so visitors are advised to rest on the first day (por la altura).","examples":[{"target":"Es mejor que saludes a todos en la fiesta.","base":"It’s better to greet everyone at the party."},{"target":"Te aconsejo que descanses el primer día.","base":"I advise you to rest on the first day."}]},"takeaways":["You compare greetings and punctuality in different countries.","You give a visitor advice about places and good manners."]},{"id":"es-b1-u06-listen","unit_id":"es-b1-u06","position":8,"title_es":"Listening practice","objective_es":"Recognize the words and phrases of the unit by ear.","intro":null,"takeaways":[]},{"id":"es-b1-u06-words","unit_id":"es-b1-u06","position":9,"title_es":"Words of the unit","objective_es":"Fix the vocabulary of the unit: recognize it and write it.","intro":null,"takeaways":[]},{"id":"es-b1-u06-final","unit_id":"es-b1-u06","position":10,"title_es":"Final review","objective_es":"Show everything from “Travel and culture”.","intro":null,"takeaways":[]},{"id":"es-b1-u07-l01","unit_id":"es-b1-u07","position":1,"title_es":"Yo que tú, descansaría","objective_es":"Give advice with yo que tú, yo en tu lugar and sería mejor.","intro":{"explanation":"You already give advice with deberías + infinitive (deberías descansar). To sound like a friend who puts themselves in your place, Spanish says yo que tú (if I were you) or yo en tu lugar (in your place) + a verb in the conditional. The conditional is the whole infinitive + -ía: descansar → descansaría, comer → comería, dormir → dormiría. The í has a written accent, and the stress falls on it: des-can-sa-RÍ-a. Sería mejor (it would be better) goes with an infinitive: sería mejor ir al médico.","examples":[{"target":"Estás agotado. Yo que tú, descansaría un poco.","base":"You’re exhausted. If I were you, I’d rest a little."},{"target":"Sería mejor ir al médico hoy.","base":"It would be better to go to the doctor today."}]},"takeaways":["You give advice with yo que tú or yo en tu lugar + a verb ending in -ía.","You suggest a better option with sería mejor + infinitive."]},{"id":"es-b1-u07-l02","unit_id":"es-b1-u07","position":2,"title_es":"Una dieta sana","objective_es":"Talk about diet, exercise, weight and smoking.","intro":{"explanation":"La dieta is what you usually eat (una dieta sana, a healthy diet); estar a dieta means to be on a diet. Sano means healthy and agrees like any adjective: una vida sana; comer sano (to eat healthily) is also very common. To exercise is hacer ejercicio, with no article. To lose weight is bajar de peso (the opposite is subir de peso). And to stop smoking is dejar de fumar: dejé de fumar hace un año.","examples":[{"target":"Hago ejercicio tres veces por semana.","base":"I exercise three times a week."},{"target":"Quiero bajar de peso: voy a comer más sano.","base":"I want to lose weight: I’m going to eat more healthily."}]},"takeaways":["You talk about your diet and say you exercise with hacer ejercicio.","You say someone wants to lose weight or stopped smoking."]},{"id":"es-b1-u07-rev1","unit_id":"es-b1-u07","position":3,"title_es":"Review 1","objective_es":"Consolidate “Yo que tú, descansaría” and “Una dieta sana”.","intro":null,"takeaways":[]},{"id":"es-b1-u07-l03","unit_id":"es-b1-u07","position":4,"title_es":"Cuanto más duermes…","objective_es":"Say that two things grow together with cuanto más… and cada vez más.","intro":{"explanation":"To say that one thing grows with another, Spanish uses cuanto más + verb or noun, and then más, menos, mejor or peor: cuanto más duermo, mejor me siento (the more I sleep, the better I feel). There is no word for “the”. With a noun, cuanto agrees: cuanto más café, cuanta más agua. In Mexico people often say mientras más with the same meaning. To say “more and more”, use cada vez más: tengo cada vez más estrés. Dormir changes o to ue: duermo, duermes, duerme.","examples":[{"target":"Cuanto más duermo, mejor me siento.","base":"The more I sleep, the better I feel."},{"target":"Tengo cada vez más estrés.","base":"I have more and more stress."}]},"takeaways":["You say the more…, the better… with cuanto más…, mejor…","You talk about stress and sleep, and say cada vez más for more and more."]},{"id":"es-b1-u07-l04","unit_id":"es-b1-u07","position":5,"title_es":"Me preocupa que trabajes tanto","objective_es":"Express worry with me preocupa que + subjunctive and talk about looking after yourself.","intro":{"explanation":"When you react to what another person does, the verb after que goes in the subjunctive: me preocupa que trabajes tanto (it worries me that you work so much). The subjunctive swaps the vowel of the present: trabajas → trabajes, fumas → fumes, duermes → duermas. If you talk about yourself, use the infinitive: me preocupa dormir poco. Cuidarse (to look after oneself) is reflexive: me cuido, te cuidas, and to a friend, ¡cuídate! (take care!). Meditar is to meditate.","examples":[{"target":"Me preocupa que no duermas bien.","base":"It worries me that you don’t sleep well."},{"target":"Tienes que cuidarte más.","base":"You have to look after yourself more."}]},"takeaways":["You say what worries you about someone with me preocupa que + subjunctive.","You tell a friend to look after themselves and talk about meditating."]},{"id":"es-b1-u07-rev2","unit_id":"es-b1-u07","position":6,"title_es":"Review 2","objective_es":"Consolidate “Cuanto más duermes…” and “Me preocupa que trabajes tanto”.","intro":null,"takeaways":[]},{"id":"es-b1-u07-l05","unit_id":"es-b1-u07","position":7,"title_es":"Me alegra que te cuides","objective_es":"React to good news with me alegra que + subjunctive and talk about well-being.","intro":{"explanation":"Me alegra que (I’m glad that) works like me preocupa que: when someone else does the action, the verb goes in the subjunctive. Estar becomes estés, hacer becomes hagas, cuidarse becomes te cuides: me alegra que estés mejor. You will also hear me alegro de que and, very often in Mexico, ¡qué bueno que…! with the same subjunctive. El bienestar (well-being) is more than health: sleep, free time, friends.","examples":[{"target":"Me alegra que estés mejor.","base":"I’m glad you’re feeling better."},{"target":"Para mi bienestar, necesito tiempo libre.","base":"For my well-being, I need free time."}]},"takeaways":["You react to good news with me alegra que + subjunctive.","You talk about well-being and encourage a friend."]},{"id":"es-b1-u07-listen","unit_id":"es-b1-u07","position":8,"title_es":"Listening practice","objective_es":"Recognize the words and phrases of the unit by ear.","intro":null,"takeaways":[]},{"id":"es-b1-u07-words","unit_id":"es-b1-u07","position":9,"title_es":"Words of the unit","objective_es":"Fix the vocabulary of the unit: recognize it and write it.","intro":null,"takeaways":[]},{"id":"es-b1-u07-final","unit_id":"es-b1-u07","position":10,"title_es":"Final review","objective_es":"Show everything from “Health and well-being”.","intro":null,"takeaways":[]},{"id":"es-b1-u08-l01","unit_id":"es-b1-u08","position":1,"title_es":"La app que uso","objective_es":"Describe apps and places with que and donde.","intro":{"explanation":"Que joins two ideas and refers to people or things: es la app que uso (it’s the app I use). English often drops “that”, but Spanish never drops que. For a place, use donde, without an accent: la escuela donde estudio. With an accent, ¿dónde? is the question word; the sound is almost the same, but in the question the word is stressed. Descargar is to download (in A2 you also learned bajar), and a user is el usuario or la usuaria.","examples":[{"target":"Es la app que uso todos los días.","base":"It’s the app I use every day."},{"target":"Es la app donde hablo con mi familia.","base":"It’s the app where I talk to my family."}]},"takeaways":["You describe an app or a person with que, and you never leave it out.","You describe a place with donde, without an accent."]},{"id":"es-b1-u08-l02","unit_id":"es-b1-u08","position":2,"title_es":"Busco una app que funcione","objective_es":"Say what kind of app or device you are looking for, with the subjunctive.","intro":{"explanation":"After que, the verb depends on what you know. If the thing exists and you know it, use the indicative: tengo una app que guarda mis archivos. If you are looking for it and don’t know if it exists, use the subjunctive: busco una app que sea gratis, necesito una computadora que funcione bien. Guardar is to save (a file) and also to keep; actualizar is to update. Remember that a pronoun can go at the end of an infinitive: voy a actualizarla = la voy a actualizar.","examples":[{"target":"Tengo una app que guarda mis archivos.","base":"I have an app that saves my files."},{"target":"Busco una app que sea gratis.","base":"I’m looking for an app that’s free."}]},"takeaways":["You describe something you know with que + indicative (tengo una app que funciona).","You describe something you are looking for with que + subjunctive (busco una app que funcione)."]},{"id":"es-b1-u08-rev1","unit_id":"es-b1-u08","position":3,"title_es":"Review 1","objective_es":"Consolidate “La app que uso” and “Busco una app que funcione”.","intro":null,"takeaways":[]},{"id":"es-b1-u08-l03","unit_id":"es-b1-u08","position":4,"title_es":"Lo que pasa es que…","objective_es":"Explain a technical problem with lo que and understand the solution.","intro":{"explanation":"Lo que means “what” when it is not a question: it is “the thing that”. Lo que necesito es un celular nuevo (what I need is a new phone). Lo que pasa es que… (the thing is…) is the natural way to start explaining a problem, for example on the phone with technical support. Don’t mix it up with ¿qué?, which is only for questions. For a phone, cargar is to charge (lo cargo toda la noche) and la batería is the battery; borrar is to delete.","examples":[{"target":"Lo que pasa es que mi celular no carga bien.","base":"The thing is, my phone isn’t charging properly."},{"target":"Lo que necesito es un celular nuevo.","base":"What I need is a new phone."}]},"takeaways":["You explain a problem with lo que pasa es que…","You talk about the battery, charging and deleting apps."]},{"id":"es-b1-u08-l04","unit_id":"es-b1-u08","position":5,"title_es":"En las redes sociales","objective_es":"Talk about what you post and share, and point to things with el que / la que.","intro":{"explanation":"El que, la que, los que and las que mean “the one(s) that”. They agree with the noun you don’t repeat: ¿Qué foto? — La que publiqué ayer (la foto). On social media (las redes sociales) you publicar (post), compartir (share) and leave un comentario. For people after a preposition you can also use quien: el amigo con quien hablo (= con el que hablo).","examples":[{"target":"¿Qué foto? — La que publiqué ayer.","base":"Which photo? — The one I posted yesterday."},{"target":"Paso mucho tiempo en las redes sociales.","base":"I spend a lot of time on social media."}]},"takeaways":["You talk about posting, sharing and comments on social media.","You say “the one that” with el que, la que, los que, las que."]},{"id":"es-b1-u08-rev2","unit_id":"es-b1-u08","position":6,"title_es":"Review 2","objective_es":"Consolidate “Lo que pasa es que…” and “En las redes sociales”.","intro":null,"takeaways":[]},{"id":"es-b1-u08-l05","unit_id":"es-b1-u08","position":7,"title_es":"Te mando el enlace","objective_es":"Send and share things with object pronouns and understand online-class instructions.","intro":{"explanation":"To share a link or a file, Spanish usually says mandar: te mando el enlace (in Mexico you will also hear el link). With two pronouns, the person goes first: te lo mando (I’ll send it to you). When le or les meets lo or la, le becomes se: ¿El enlace para Diego? Se lo mando ahora. Quien (no accent) can mean “whoever”: quien no reciba el enlace puede escribirme.","examples":[{"target":"¿El enlace? Te lo mando ahora.","base":"The link? I’ll send it to you now."},{"target":"¿Y el archivo para Diego? Se lo mando por correo.","base":"And the file for Diego? I’ll send it to him by email."}]},"takeaways":["You send and share things with te lo mando and se lo mando.","You understand instructions for an online class."]},{"id":"es-b1-u08-listen","unit_id":"es-b1-u08","position":8,"title_es":"Listening practice","objective_es":"Recognize the words and phrases of the unit by ear.","intro":null,"takeaways":[]},{"id":"es-b1-u08-words","unit_id":"es-b1-u08","position":9,"title_es":"Words of the unit","objective_es":"Fix the vocabulary of the unit: recognize it and write it.","intro":null,"takeaways":[]},{"id":"es-b1-u08-final","unit_id":"es-b1-u08","position":10,"title_es":"Final review","objective_es":"Show everything from “Technology and communication”.","intro":null,"takeaways":[]},{"id":"es-b1-u09-l01","unit_id":"es-b1-u09","position":1,"title_es":"El medio ambiente","objective_es":"Talk about nature and the main environmental problems.","intro":{"explanation":"El medio ambiente (the environment) is two words and always takes the article el: proteger el medio ambiente. La naturaleza is nature in general, and el bosque a forest. La contaminación (pollution) comes from contaminar (to pollute), a regular -ar verb: los carros contaminan el aire. Words in -ción are always feminine and lose the accent in the plural: la contaminación. Climate change is el cambio climático: in Spanish the adjective goes after the noun. Proteger (to protect) changes g to j only before o and a: protejo (I protect), but proteges, protege, protegemos. Pronunciation: contaminación is stressed on the last syllable (con-ta-mi-na-CIÓN), and the c before i sounds like s in Latin America.","examples":[{"target":"Los carros contaminan el aire de la ciudad.","base":"Cars pollute the city’s air."},{"target":"Tenemos que proteger la naturaleza.","base":"We have to protect nature."}]},"takeaways":["You name nature and the environment: el medio ambiente, la naturaleza, el bosque.","You talk about problems like la contaminación and el cambio climático."]},{"id":"es-b1-u09-l02","unit_id":"es-b1-u09","position":2,"title_es":"Se recicla","objective_es":"Say what is done, in general, with se + verb: se recicla, se separan.","intro":{"explanation":"To say what is done without saying who does it, Spanish uses se + a verb in the third person. If the noun is singular, the verb is singular: se recicla el papel (paper is recycled). If the noun is plural, the verb is plural: se reciclan las botellas, se separan las bolsas. Without a noun, se + verb means “people / you” in general: en mi barrio se recicla mucho (people recycle a lot in my neighborhood). La basura is uncountable, like trash: la basura (never “las basuras” for “trash”). In Mexico the trash can is el bote de basura. Pronunciation: in reciclaje, the c before i sounds like s (re-si-CLA-je) and the j is like a strong English h. Many Mexican neighborhoods now separate trash into organic (orgánica) and inorganic (inorgánica).","examples":[{"target":"En mi barrio se recicla mucho más que antes.","base":"People recycle much more than before in my neighborhood."},{"target":"Acá se reciclan el papel y el plástico.","base":"Paper and plastic are recycled here."}]},"takeaways":["You say what is done with se + verb: se recicla el papel, se reciclan las botellas.","You talk about trash and recycling: la basura, el plástico, el reciclaje."]},{"id":"es-b1-u09-rev1","unit_id":"es-b1-u09","position":3,"title_es":"Review 1","objective_es":"Consolidate “El medio ambiente” and “Se recicla”.","intro":null,"takeaways":[]},{"id":"es-b1-u09-l03","unit_id":"es-b1-u09","position":4,"title_es":"Fue creado por…","objective_es":"Use the passive with ser + participle and talk about volunteering.","intro":{"explanation":"Spanish also has a passive like the English one: ser + participle + por (by). Fue (was) is the preterite of ser: el bosque fue limpiado por 200 voluntarios. The participle agrees like an adjective: el mercado fue creado, la plaza fue limpiada, los árboles fueron plantados. This passive is more typical of news, posters and written texts; in conversation people usually say se plantaron 300 árboles or los voluntarios plantaron 300 árboles. A volunteer is el voluntario or la voluntaria, and la comunidad is the community. Gastar means to spend money, but also to use up water or energy: gastar menos energía. La energía has a stressed í: e-ner-GÍ-a.","examples":[{"target":"El bosque fue limpiado por 200 voluntarios.","base":"The forest was cleaned by 200 volunteers."},{"target":"En casa gastamos menos energía que antes.","base":"At home we use less energy than before."}]},"takeaways":["You use the passive with fue / fueron + participle + por.","You talk about volunteers, the community and using less energy."]},{"id":"es-b1-u09-l04","unit_id":"es-b1-u09","position":5,"title_es":"No creo que sea…","objective_es":"Give a nuanced opinion: creo que + indicative, no creo que and dudo que + subjunctive.","intro":{"explanation":"Creo que (I think that) is followed by the normal verb, the indicative: creo que es la fábrica. When you deny or doubt something, Spanish uses the subjunctive: no creo que sea la fábrica (I don’t think it’s the factory), dudo que la ciudad haga algo (I doubt the city will do anything). The present subjunctive swaps the typical vowel: -ar verbs take e (cambiar → cambie, reciclar → recicle), -er / -ir verbs take a (hacer → haga, tener → tenga, poder → pueda). Ser → sea and haber → haya are irregular. A useful way to think about it: creo que states something as true, while no creo que and dudo que talk about something you do not accept as true. To sound polite in a discussion, start softly: Mmm, no creo que…","examples":[{"target":"Creo que es la fábrica.","base":"I think it’s the factory."},{"target":"No creo que sea la fábrica.","base":"I don’t think it’s the factory."}]},"takeaways":["You say what you think with creo que + indicative (creo que es).","You say what you don’t think or doubt with no creo que / dudo que + subjunctive (no creo que sea)."]},{"id":"es-b1-u09-rev2","unit_id":"es-b1-u09","position":6,"title_es":"Review 2","objective_es":"Consolidate “Fue creado por…” and “No creo que sea…”.","intro":null,"takeaways":[]},{"id":"es-b1-u09-l05","unit_id":"es-b1-u09","position":7,"title_es":"Mi barrio y el medio ambiente","objective_es":"Describe what is done in your neighborhood and give a nuanced opinion about it.","intro":{"explanation":"To describe your neighborhood, combine everything: what is done (se recicla, se venden, no se usa plástico), what was done (fue creado por…, se plantaron árboles) and your opinion (creo que…, no creo que…, dudo que…). Connectors help you build a short argument: también (also), sin embargo (however), por eso (that’s why). A balanced opinion often has two halves: creo que es una buena idea, pero dudo que sea suficiente.","examples":[{"target":"En mi barrio se recicla, pero no creo que sea suficiente.","base":"People recycle in my neighborhood, but I don’t think it’s enough."},{"target":"La calle también fue limpiada por los voluntarios.","base":"The street was also cleaned by the volunteers."}]},"takeaways":["You describe what is done in your neighborhood with se and the passive.","You give a balanced opinion with creo que, no creo que and dudo que."]},{"id":"es-b1-u09-listen","unit_id":"es-b1-u09","position":8,"title_es":"Listening practice","objective_es":"Recognize the words and phrases of the unit by ear.","intro":null,"takeaways":[]},{"id":"es-b1-u09-words","unit_id":"es-b1-u09","position":9,"title_es":"Words of the unit","objective_es":"Fix the vocabulary of the unit: recognize it and write it.","intro":null,"takeaways":[]},{"id":"es-b1-u09-final","unit_id":"es-b1-u09","position":10,"title_es":"Final review","objective_es":"Show everything from “Environment and society”.","intro":null,"takeaways":[]},{"id":"es-b1-u10-l01","unit_id":"es-b1-u10","position":1,"title_es":"Las noticias de hoy","objective_es":"Understand headlines and talk about the news and the media you use.","intro":{"explanation":"In Spanish, news is plural: las noticias son buenas (the news is good). One piece of news is una noticia. A newspaper is el periódico (also el diario in many countries), and a headline is el titular. Periódico and artículo are stressed on the third syllable from the end, so they carry a written accent: pe-RIÓ-di-co, ar-TÍ-cu-lo. El incendio is a fire that destroys something (a house, a forest); for fire in general, Spanish says el fuego. La tormenta is a storm. El gobierno can be national or local: el gobierno de la ciudad. Headlines are short and often have no verb and no full stop: Tormenta en Veracruz.","examples":[{"target":"Leo el periódico todas las mañanas.","base":"I read the newspaper every morning."},{"target":"Hubo un incendio en una fábrica del centro.","base":"There was a fire in a factory downtown."}]},"takeaways":["You talk about las noticias, el periódico, los artículos and los titulares.","You understand headlines about a storm, a fire or the government."]},{"id":"es-b1-u10-l02","unit_id":"es-b1-u10","position":2,"title_es":"Según el gobierno…","objective_es":"Say what happened and who reported or announced it, with según.","intro":{"explanation":"Según means “according to”. It goes before a noun or a subject pronoun: según el periódico, según el gobierno, según ella, según tú (not “según ti”). Ocurrir means to happen, like pasar, but it sounds a bit more formal and is very common in the news: ¿qué ocurrió? Anunciar (to announce) and informar (to report, to inform) are regular -ar verbs: el gobierno anunció…, nos informa Tomás. A journalist is el periodista or la periodista: words in -ista are the same for men and women, only the article changes. Remember that la radio (the medium) is feminine; in Mexico you will also hear el radio.","examples":[{"target":"Según el pronóstico, va a llover hasta el jueves.","base":"According to the forecast, it’s going to rain until Thursday."},{"target":"El gobierno anunció que hoy no hay clases.","base":"The government announced that there are no classes today."}]},"takeaways":["You say where information comes from with según.","You talk about what happened and who announced or reported it: ocurrir, anunciar, informar."]},{"id":"es-b1-u10-rev1","unit_id":"es-b1-u10","position":3,"title_es":"Review 1","objective_es":"Consolidate “Las noticias de hoy” and “Según el gobierno…”.","intro":null,"takeaways":[]},{"id":"es-b1-u10-l03","unit_id":"es-b1-u10","position":4,"title_es":"Explicó que era…","objective_es":"Report what someone said in the past, moving the verb back in time.","intro":{"explanation":"When you report something said in the past, the verb usually moves one step back, as in English: “Es la tormenta más fuerte del año” → explicó que era la tormenta más fuerte del año. Present → imperfect (es → era, tiene → tenía); va a → iba a (dijo que iba a llover); future → conditional (vendrá → vendría, the conditional ending -ía). If the news is still true today, people in Latin America often keep the present: me dijo que está enfermo. Contar means to tell (a story, news) and also to count: me contó que… El testigo is a witness; it does not change for women: la testigo.","examples":[{"target":"Su vecino explicó que era la tormenta más fuerte del año.","base":"Her neighbor explained that it was the strongest storm of the year."},{"target":"Una testigo contó que el agua llegó a su cocina.","base":"A witness said that the water reached her kitchen."}]},"takeaways":["You report what someone said with explicó que, contó que and dijo que.","You move the verb back: es → era, va a → iba a, vendrá → vendría."]},{"id":"es-b1-u10-l04","unit_id":"es-b1-u10","position":5,"title_es":"Me preguntó si…","objective_es":"Report questions with preguntó si / dónde and requests with pidió que.","intro":{"explanation":"To report a yes / no question, use preguntar + si (if): “¿Tienes tiempo?” → me preguntó si tenía tiempo. With a question word, keep the word and its accent: “¿Dónde vives?” → me preguntó dónde vivía. Preguntar is to ask a question; pedir is to ask for something or to ask someone to do something. Pedir que is followed by the subjunctive: me pide que llame (she asks me to call). After me pidió que, Latin Americans usually keep the present subjunctive if the request is still valid: me pidió que la llame mañana, me pidió que cierre las ventanas. In written Spanish you will also see me pidió que la llamara (imperfect subjunctive): just recognize it for now. Pedir changes e to i: pido, pides, pide, pidió.","examples":[{"target":"Me preguntó si tenía tiempo.","base":"She asked me if I had time."},{"target":"Mi mamá me pidió que la llame esta noche.","base":"My mom asked me to call her tonight."}]},"takeaways":["You report questions with me preguntó si… and me preguntó dónde / cuándo…","You report requests with me pidió que + subjunctive."]},{"id":"es-b1-u10-rev2","unit_id":"es-b1-u10","position":6,"title_es":"Review 2","objective_es":"Consolidate “Explicó que era…” and “Me preguntó si…”.","intro":null,"takeaways":[]},{"id":"es-b1-u10-l05","unit_id":"es-b1-u10","position":7,"title_es":"Te cuento la noticia","objective_es":"Tell a piece of news and pass on what different people said, asked and asked for.","intro":{"explanation":"To pass on news, start with te cuento (let me tell you) or ¿sabes qué pasó?, then report each person with the right verb: dijo que, explicó que, contó que for statements; me preguntó si / dónde for questions; me pidió que + subjunctive for requests. Add según to say where the information comes from. A news story usually answers: ¿qué ocurrió?, ¿dónde?, ¿cuándo? and ¿qué dicen los testigos?","examples":[{"target":"Te cuento lo que pasó en la reunión de hoy.","base":"Let me tell you what happened at today’s meeting."},{"target":"Según ella, la fecha límite es el viernes.","base":"According to her, the deadline is Friday."}]},"takeaways":["You tell a news story: what happened, where, when and what the witnesses said.","You pass on what people said, asked and asked you to do."]},{"id":"es-b1-u10-listen","unit_id":"es-b1-u10","position":8,"title_es":"Listening practice","objective_es":"Recognize the words and phrases of the unit by ear.","intro":null,"takeaways":[]},{"id":"es-b1-u10-words","unit_id":"es-b1-u10","position":9,"title_es":"Words of the unit","objective_es":"Fix the vocabulary of the unit: recognize it and write it.","intro":null,"takeaways":[]},{"id":"es-b1-u10-final","unit_id":"es-b1-u10","position":10,"title_es":"Final review","objective_es":"Show everything from “News and media”.","intro":null,"takeaways":[]},{"id":"es-b1-u11-l01","unit_id":"es-b1-u11","position":1,"title_es":"Ventajas y desventajas","objective_es":"Give your opinion and weigh up the advantages and disadvantages of something.","intro":{"explanation":"Opinar means to give an opinion: ¿quién quiere opinar? To weigh up a topic, list la ventaja (advantage) and la desventaja (disadvantage): both are feminine (las ventajas, las desventajas), and des- works like English dis-. Organize two arguments with por un lado… (on the one hand) and por otro lado… (on the other hand): por un lado opens the first argument and por otro lado the second, usually after a comma or a semicolon. Keep using what you learned in A2 to frame your opinion: creo que, me parece que, para mí. Pronunciation: v sounds exactly like b, and j is a strong breathy sound: ven-TA-ja.","examples":[{"target":"Por un lado, la ciudad es cara; por otro lado, hay mucho trabajo.","base":"On the one hand, the city is expensive; on the other hand, there is a lot of work."},{"target":"Una desventaja del campo es que no hay cines.","base":"One disadvantage of the countryside is that there are no cinemas."}]},"takeaways":["You give your opinion and talk about la ventaja and la desventaja.","You organize two arguments with por un lado… por otro lado…."]},{"id":"es-b1-u11-l02","unit_id":"es-b1-u11","position":2,"title_es":"Estoy en contra · en cambio","objective_es":"Say you are against something, contrast two points of view and talk about arguing and convincing.","intro":{"explanation":"Estar en contra de means to be against something. Conjugate estar (estoy, estás, está en contra) and remember that de + el becomes del: estoy en contra del plan. En cambio contrasts two people or things (whereas, by contrast): mi hermano prefiere el campo; yo, en cambio, prefiero la ciudad. It often goes right after the second subject, between commas. Don’t mix it up with por otro lado, which adds a second argument to the same opinion. Discutir is a false friend: it usually means to argue (to have a disagreement), and only sometimes to discuss a topic calmly; context tells you which. Convencer means to convince: ¿Valeria te convenció? (did Valeria convince you?), ¡me convenciste! (you convinced me!). In Mexico and much of Latin America a direct “no” can sound rude, so people soften a disagreement: tienes razón, pero…","examples":[{"target":"Estoy en contra de trabajar los domingos.","base":"I’m against working on Sundays."},{"target":"Andrés prefiere el campo; Paula, en cambio, prefiere la ciudad.","base":"Andrés prefers the countryside, whereas Paula prefers the city."}]},"takeaways":["You say you are against something with estar en contra de.","You contrast two points of view with en cambio and talk about discutir and convencer."]},{"id":"es-b1-u11-rev1","unit_id":"es-b1-u11","position":3,"title_es":"Review 1","objective_es":"Consolidate “Ventajas y desventajas” and “Estoy en contra · en cambio”.","intro":null,"takeaways":[]},{"id":"es-b1-u11-l03","unit_id":"es-b1-u11","position":4,"title_es":"Si tuviera…, iría","objective_es":"Imagine unreal situations with si + imperfect subjunctive and the conditional.","intro":{"explanation":"To talk about something unreal or unlikely, use si + imperfect subjunctive, then the conditional: si tuviera dinero, iría a Italia (if I had money, I would go to Italy). The imperfect subjunctive comes from the ellos form of the preterite: tuvieron → tuviera, pudieron → pudiera, fueron → fuera, estuvieron → estuviera, vivieron → viviera. The yo and él / ella forms are the same (si yo tuviera, si ella tuviera); for tú, add -s: si tuvieras. The conditional adds -ía to the infinitive (viviría, trabajaría, iría), and a few verbs have an irregular stem: hacer → haría, tener → tendría, poder → podría; for tú, add -s again: harías, irías. Never use the conditional after si: si tuviera, not si tendría. Listen to the stress: tu-VIE-ra, pu-DIE-ra, but i-RÍ-a, ha-RÍ-a: the written accent keeps the i and the a in separate syllables.","examples":[{"target":"Si tuviera dinero, iría a Italia.","base":"If I had money, I would go to Italy."},{"target":"Si pudiera, trabajaría menos horas.","base":"If I could, I would work fewer hours."}]},"takeaways":["You imagine unreal situations with si tuviera, si pudiera or si fuera + the conditional.","You form the imperfect subjunctive from the preterite: tuvieron → tuviera."]},{"id":"es-b1-u11-l04","unit_id":"es-b1-u11","position":5,"title_es":"Si yo fuera tú…","objective_es":"Give advice with si yo fuera tú and talk about friendship, trust and respect.","intro":{"explanation":"Si yo fuera tú (if I were you) + the conditional is a gentle, very common way to give advice: si yo fuera tú, hablaría con él. Fuera comes from ser here (fueron → fuera), so it covers both “if I were” and “if it were”. You already know two other ways to say it: yo que tú and en tu lugar (en tu lugar, no iría). Confiar (to trust) needs en: confiar en alguien, puedes confiar en mí. In the present it has a written accent on the í: confío, confías, confía. La amistad (friendship) is feminine, like most nouns in -dad (la ciudad), while el respeto (respect) is masculine. With all due respect is con todo respeto, a polite way to introduce a disagreement.","examples":[{"target":"Si yo fuera tú, hablaría con ella.","base":"If I were you, I would talk to her."},{"target":"Puedes confiar en mí.","base":"You can trust me."}]},"takeaways":["You give advice with si yo fuera tú + the conditional.","You talk about la amistad, el respeto and confiar en alguien."]},{"id":"es-b1-u11-rev2","unit_id":"es-b1-u11","position":6,"title_es":"Review 2","objective_es":"Consolidate “Si tuviera…, iría” and “Si yo fuera tú…”.","intro":null,"takeaways":[]},{"id":"es-b1-u11-l05","unit_id":"es-b1-u11","position":7,"title_es":"¿Qué harías tú?","objective_es":"Defend an opinion with arguments, react politely to other opinions and imagine alternatives.","intro":{"explanation":"Now put it all together: present the topic, give arguments with por un lado… por otro lado…, compare with en cambio, take a position (estoy de acuerdo, estoy en contra) and imagine alternatives with si + imperfect subjunctive. In a discussion, react to the other person before you disagree: tienes razón, pero… or, more formally, con todo respeto… The si half can also go second, with no comma: iría si pudiera. In a hypothesis, the voice usually rises at the end of the si half and falls at the end of the sentence: si tuviera tiempo (↗), iría (↘).","examples":[{"target":"Tienes razón, pero si yo fuera el jefe, no haría eso.","base":"You’re right, but if I were the boss, I wouldn’t do that."},{"target":"Por un lado, me encanta la ciudad; por otro lado, si pudiera, viviría en el campo.","base":"On the one hand, I love the city; on the other hand, if I could, I would live in the countryside."}]},"takeaways":["You defend an opinion with arguments and connectors.","You disagree politely and imagine what you would do in a different situation."]},{"id":"es-b1-u11-listen","unit_id":"es-b1-u11","position":8,"title_es":"Listening practice","objective_es":"Recognize the words and phrases of the unit by ear.","intro":null,"takeaways":[]},{"id":"es-b1-u11-words","unit_id":"es-b1-u11","position":9,"title_es":"Words of the unit","objective_es":"Fix the vocabulary of the unit: recognize it and write it.","intro":null,"takeaways":[]},{"id":"es-b1-u11-final","unit_id":"es-b1-u11","position":10,"title_es":"Final review","objective_es":"Show everything from “Opinions and relationships”.","intro":null,"takeaways":[]},{"id":"es-b1-u12-l01","unit_id":"es-b1-u12","position":1,"title_es":"Ya que · por lo tanto","objective_es":"Explain the cause and the consequence of a problem.","intro":{"explanation":"Ya que (since, because) introduces a cause the listener knows or can accept, and it often opens the sentence: ya que llueve, nos quedamos en casa. Porque does a similar job but usually comes after the main idea. Por lo tanto (therefore) introduces the consequence; it is more formal than por eso and very common in emails and complaints. In the middle of a sentence, put a semicolon or a comma before it and a comma after it: está dañado; por lo tanto, no funciona. Dañado (damaged) agrees like any adjective: el celular está dañado, la puerta está dañada. Arreglar means to fix or repair; in Mexico you will also hear componer.","examples":[{"target":"Ya que la computadora está dañada, no puedo trabajar.","base":"Since the computer is damaged, I can’t work."},{"target":"El carro está dañado; por lo tanto, voy en autobús.","base":"The car is damaged; therefore, I’m going by bus."}]},"takeaways":["You explain a cause with ya que and a consequence with por lo tanto.","You say that something is dañado and needs to be fixed (arreglar)."]},{"id":"es-b1-u12-l02","unit_id":"es-b1-u12","position":2,"title_es":"Aunque · a pesar de","objective_es":"Admit a fact that doesn’t change the result, and talk about warranties and repairs.","intro":{"explanation":"Aunque (although) + indicative admits a real fact that does not change the result: aunque es nueva, la lavadora no funciona. The aunque part can go first or second: no funciona, aunque es nueva. A pesar de (in spite of, despite) is followed by a noun or an infinitive, never by a conjugated verb: a pesar de la lluvia, a pesar de tener garantía. De + el contracts here too: a pesar del precio. El técnico (la técnica for a woman) is the person who repairs appliances, computers or phones. La garantía is the warranty: tiene un año de garantía, la garantía no incluye la pantalla (the warranty doesn’t cover the screen). Pronunciation: garantía has a hard g, as in go (g before a, o, u), and the written accent puts the stress on the í: ga-ran-TÍ-a. Técnico is stressed on the first syllable: TÉC-ni-co.","examples":[{"target":"Aunque es nueva, la lavadora no funciona.","base":"Although it’s new, the washing machine doesn’t work."},{"target":"A pesar de la garantía, tengo que pagar.","base":"In spite of the warranty, I have to pay."}]},"takeaways":["You admit a fact that doesn’t change the result with aunque + indicative.","You use a pesar de + a noun or an infinitive, and talk about el técnico and la garantía."]},{"id":"es-b1-u12-rev1","unit_id":"es-b1-u12","position":3,"title_es":"Review 1","objective_es":"Consolidate “Ya que · por lo tanto” and “Aunque · a pesar de”.","intro":null,"takeaways":[]},{"id":"es-b1-u12-l03","unit_id":"es-b1-u12","position":4,"title_es":"A lo mejor, quizás, seguramente","objective_es":"Guess the cause of a problem and say how likely a solution is.","intro":{"explanation":"To guess, Spanish has several options. A lo mejor (maybe) is very common in conversation and always takes the indicative: a lo mejor es el vecino. Quizás (perhaps; also written quizá) usually takes the subjunctive when it comes before the verb: quizás pueda (perhaps I can), quizás sea el vecino (perhaps it’s the neighbor), although you will also hear the indicative. Seguramente looks like “surely”, but it really means probably, most likely: seguramente es la música (it’s probably the music). Resolver (to solve) changes o to ue in the present (resuelvo, resuelves, resuelve) and has a regular preterite: resolvió. A lo mejor is said as one unit, with the stress at the end: a-lo-me-JOR.","examples":[{"target":"A lo mejor es la música del vecino.","base":"Maybe it’s the neighbor’s music."},{"target":"Seguramente el técnico puede resolver el problema.","base":"The technician can probably solve the problem."}]},"takeaways":["You guess with a lo mejor (+ indicative), quizás (+ subjunctive) and seguramente.","You talk about how to resolver un problema."]},{"id":"es-b1-u12-l04","unit_id":"es-b1-u12","position":5,"title_es":"Les escribo para quejarme","objective_es":"Make a formal complaint and ask for a refund.","intro":{"explanation":"Quejarse (to complain) is reflexive and takes de: me quejo, te quejas, se queja del ruido; with an infinitive, the pronoun goes at the end: para quejarme. In a formal email or call, use usted (one person) or ustedes (a company, a hotel) and fixed phrases: Estimados señores: (Dear Sir or Madam), Les escribo para quejarme de… (I am writing to complain about…), Solicito un reembolso (I request a refund) and Atentamente (Sincerely) before your name. La queja is the complaint itself. Reclamar means to claim what you are owed (reclamar un reembolso, reclamar el dinero) and, in many countries, to complain formally; you usually do it at el servicio al cliente (customer service). Be firm but polite: give the facts, the cause (ya que…) and what you want (por lo tanto, solicito…).","examples":[{"target":"Les escribo para quejarme del servicio.","base":"I am writing to complain about the service."},{"target":"Solicito un reembolso, ya que la lavadora llegó dañada.","base":"I request a refund, since the washing machine arrived damaged."}]},"takeaways":["You make a formal complaint with usted and the set phrases of a complaint email.","You ask for un reembolso and deal with el servicio al cliente."]},{"id":"es-b1-u12-rev2","unit_id":"es-b1-u12","position":6,"title_es":"Review 2","objective_es":"Consolidate “A lo mejor, quizás, seguramente” and “Les escribo para quejarme”.","intro":null,"takeaways":[]},{"id":"es-b1-u12-l05","unit_id":"es-b1-u12","position":7,"title_es":"Negociar una solución","objective_es":"Explain a problem, negotiate a solution and reach an agreement politely.","intro":{"explanation":"To negotiate, describe the problem (llegó dañado, no funciona), give the reason for your request (ya que…), admit what is fair (aunque…, a pesar de…) and say what you want (por lo tanto, solicito… / prefiero…). Listen to the other side and guess or propose politely: a lo mejor…, quizás…, seguramente podemos… Customer service usually speaks to you with usted, and you should answer the same way; with a neighbor or a friend, use tú. A soft opening (perdón, disculpe, ¿tiene un momento?) makes the conversation easier, and you already know how to accept a proposal: de acuerdo, perfecto, tienes razón.","examples":[{"target":"Aunque tiene garantía, prefiero un reembolso.","base":"Although it has a warranty, I prefer a refund."},{"target":"A lo mejor podemos encontrar una solución.","base":"Maybe we can find a solution."}]},"takeaways":["You explain a problem and negotiate a solution with connectors.","You choose usted or tú, and guess or propose politely."]},{"id":"es-b1-u12-listen","unit_id":"es-b1-u12","position":8,"title_es":"Listening practice","objective_es":"Recognize the words and phrases of the unit by ear.","intro":null,"takeaways":[]},{"id":"es-b1-u12-words","unit_id":"es-b1-u12","position":9,"title_es":"Words of the unit","objective_es":"Fix the vocabulary of the unit: recognize it and write it.","intro":null,"takeaways":[]},{"id":"es-b1-u12-final","unit_id":"es-b1-u12","position":10,"title_es":"Final review","objective_es":"Show everything from “Problems and solutions”.","intro":null,"takeaways":[]}],"exercises":[{"id":"es-b1-u01-l01-e01","lesson_id":"es-b1-u01-l01","position":1,"type":"translation_choice","skill":"vocabulary","prompt_es":"What does “he probado” mean?","payload":{"type":"translation_choice","word":"he probado","options":["I have tried","I have seen","I have to try","I would try"]},"answer":"I have tried","accepted_answers":[],"explanation_es":null,"difficulty":1,"recommended_seconds":12,"words":["es-he-probado"]},{"id":"es-b1-u01-l01-e02","lesson_id":"es-b1-u01-l01","position":2,"type":"match_pairs","skill":"vocabulary","prompt_es":"Match each word with its meaning","payload":{"type":"match_pairs","pairs":[{"left":"he visto","right":"I have seen"},{"left":"he probado","right":"I have tried"},{"left":"la experiencia","right":"experience"},{"left":"en mi vida","right":"in my life"}]},"answer":"","accepted_answers":[],"explanation_es":null,"difficulty":1,"recommended_seconds":30,"words":["es-he-visto","es-he-probado","es-la-experiencia","es-en-mi-vida"]},{"id":"es-b1-u01-l01-e03","lesson_id":"es-b1-u01-l01","position":3,"type":"fill_blank","skill":"grammar","prompt_es":"Complete the sentence","payload":{"type":"fill_blank","sentence":"¿Alguna vez ___ probado el mole?","translation":"Have you ever tried mole?","options":["has","he","ha","hemos"]},"answer":"has","accepted_answers":[],"explanation_es":"With tú, haber is has: ¿alguna vez has probado…? He is yo, ha is él / ella / usted and hemos is nosotros.","difficulty":1,"recommended_seconds":15,"words":["es-he-probado"]},{"id":"es-b1-u01-l01-e04","lesson_id":"es-b1-u01-l01","position":4,"type":"fill_blank","skill":"grammar","prompt_es":"Complete the sentence","payload":{"type":"fill_blank","sentence":"Nunca he ___ el mar.","translation":"I’ve never seen the sea.","options":["visto","vi","veo"]},"answer":"visto","accepted_answers":[],"explanation_es":"The participle of ver is irregular: visto. Vi (I saw) is the preterite and veo (I see) the present; neither can follow he.","difficulty":1,"recommended_seconds":15,"words":["es-he-visto"]},{"id":"es-b1-u01-l01-e05","lesson_id":"es-b1-u01-l01","position":5,"type":"listen_choice","skill":"listening","prompt_es":"Listen and choose what you hear","payload":{"type":"listen_choice","audioText":"Nunca he visto el mar.","options":["Nunca he visto el mar.","Nunca vi el mar.","Ya he visto el mar.","Nunca he ido al mar."]},"answer":"Nunca he visto el mar.","accepted_answers":[],"explanation_es":null,"difficulty":1,"recommended_seconds":15,"words":["es-he-visto"]},{"id":"es-b1-u01-l01-e06","lesson_id":"es-b1-u01-l01","position":6,"type":"order_words","skill":"sentence_building","prompt_es":"Put the words in order","payload":{"type":"order_words","tokens":["Nunca","he","visto","nada","tan","bonito","en","mi","vida"],"translation":"I’ve never seen anything so beautiful in my life."},"answer":"Nunca he visto nada tan bonito en mi vida.","accepted_answers":[],"explanation_es":null,"difficulty":1,"recommended_seconds":25,"words":["es-en-mi-vida","es-he-visto"]},{"id":"es-b1-u01-l01-e07","lesson_id":"es-b1-u01-l01","position":7,"type":"context_choice","skill":"listening","prompt_es":"Listen and answer","payload":{"type":"context_choice","stimulus":{"kind":"listening","id":"es.stim.b1-u1-radio","setting":"On a radio show in Mexico City, Andrés interviews Valeria, a traveler from Puebla.","speakers":[{"name":"Andrés","voice":"a"},{"name":"Valeria","voice":"b"}],"rate":0.95,"lines":[{"speaker":"Andrés","text":"Hoy está con nosotros Valeria, una viajera de Puebla. Valeria, ¿en cuántos países has estado?"},{"speaker":"Valeria","text":"En veinte, más o menos. He estado en casi toda Latinoamérica y también en Japón."},{"speaker":"Andrés","text":"¡Qué bien! ¿Y cuál ha sido la experiencia más increíble de tu vida?"},{"speaker":"Valeria","text":"En Perú vi Machu Picchu al amanecer. Nunca he visto nada tan bonito en mi vida."},{"speaker":"Andrés","text":"¿Y la comida? ¿Alguna vez has probado algo muy raro?"},{"speaker":"Valeria","text":"Sí, en Oaxaca he probado los chapulines. ¡Y me encantan!"},{"speaker":"Andrés","text":"¿Y qué país todavía no has visitado?"},{"speaker":"Valeria","text":"Todavía no he visitado Argentina, pero voy a ir el próximo año."}]},"question":"How many countries has Valeria been to?","options":["About twenty","Exactly ten","Only Mexico and Japan","About thirty"]},"answer":"About twenty","accepted_answers":[],"explanation_es":"Valeria answers “En veinte, más o menos”: about twenty, in almost all of Latin America and in Japan.","difficulty":1,"recommended_seconds":60,"words":[]},{"id":"es-b1-u01-l01-e08","lesson_id":"es-b1-u01-l01","position":8,"type":"context_choice","skill":"listening","prompt_es":"Listen and answer","payload":{"type":"context_choice","stimulus":{"kind":"listening","id":"es.stim.b1-u1-radio","setting":"On a radio show in Mexico City, Andrés interviews Valeria, a traveler from Puebla.","speakers":[{"name":"Andrés","voice":"a"},{"name":"Valeria","voice":"b"}],"rate":0.95,"lines":[{"speaker":"Andrés","text":"Hoy está con nosotros Valeria, una viajera de Puebla. Valeria, ¿en cuántos países has estado?"},{"speaker":"Valeria","text":"En veinte, más o menos. He estado en casi toda Latinoamérica y también en Japón."},{"speaker":"Andrés","text":"¡Qué bien! ¿Y cuál ha sido la experiencia más increíble de tu vida?"},{"speaker":"Valeria","text":"En Perú vi Machu Picchu al amanecer. Nunca he visto nada tan bonito en mi vida."},{"speaker":"Andrés","text":"¿Y la comida? ¿Alguna vez has probado algo muy raro?"},{"speaker":"Valeria","text":"Sí, en Oaxaca he probado los chapulines. ¡Y me encantan!"},{"speaker":"Andrés","text":"¿Y qué país todavía no has visitado?"},{"speaker":"Valeria","text":"Todavía no he visitado Argentina, pero voy a ir el próximo año."}]},"question":"What does Valeria say about Machu Picchu?","options":["She has never seen anything so beautiful in her life","She has never been there","She went there last year with her family","It was too crowded at sunrise"]},"answer":"She has never seen anything so beautiful in her life","accepted_answers":[],"explanation_es":"She says “En Perú vi Machu Picchu al amanecer. Nunca he visto nada tan bonito en mi vida.”","difficulty":1,"recommended_seconds":60,"words":[]},{"id":"es-b1-u01-l01-e09","lesson_id":"es-b1-u01-l01","position":9,"type":"translate_write","skill":"translation","prompt_es":"Translate into Spanish","payload":{"type":"translate_write","source":"Have you ever tried mole?"},"answer":"¿Alguna vez has probado el mole?","accepted_answers":["¿Alguna vez has probado mole?","¿Has probado alguna vez el mole?","¿Has probado el mole alguna vez?","¿Has probado el mole?","¿Has probado mole alguna vez?","¿Alguna vez probaste el mole?","¿Alguna vez probaste mole?","¿Alguna vez ha probado el mole?"],"explanation_es":null,"difficulty":1,"recommended_seconds":30,"words":["es-he-probado"]},{"id":"es-b1-u01-l01-e10","lesson_id":"es-b1-u01-l01","position":10,"type":"short_answer","skill":"sentence_building","prompt_es":"Answer in Spanish","payload":{"type":"short_answer","question":"¿Alguna vez has visto una película en español?","translation":"Answer about yourself with «Sí, he visto…» or «No, nunca he visto…»."},"answer":"Sí, he visto una película en español.","accepted_answers":["Sí, he visto una película en español","No, nunca he visto una película en español.","Sí, he visto muchas películas en español.","Sí, he visto varias películas en español.","No, nunca.","Sí, la he visto.","Sí, he visto una."],"explanation_es":null,"difficulty":1,"recommended_seconds":30,"words":["es-he-visto"]},{"id":"es-b1-u01-l02-e01","lesson_id":"es-b1-u01-l02","position":1,"type":"translation_choice","skill":"vocabulary","prompt_es":"What does “he escrito” mean?","payload":{"type":"translation_choice","word":"he escrito","options":["I have written","I have read","I have said","I am writing"]},"answer":"I have written","accepted_answers":[],"explanation_es":null,"difficulty":1,"recommended_seconds":12,"words":["es-he-escrito"]},{"id":"es-b1-u01-l02-e02","lesson_id":"es-b1-u01-l02","position":2,"type":"match_pairs","skill":"vocabulary","prompt_es":"Match each word with its meaning","payload":{"type":"match_pairs","pairs":[{"left":"he hecho","right":"I have done"},{"left":"he dicho","right":"I have said"},{"left":"he escrito","right":"I have written"},{"left":"he vuelto","right":"I have come back"}]},"answer":"","accepted_answers":[],"explanation_es":null,"difficulty":1,"recommended_seconds":30,"words":["es-he-hecho","es-he-dicho","es-he-escrito","es-he-vuelto"]},{"id":"es-b1-u01-l02-e03","lesson_id":"es-b1-u01-l02","position":3,"type":"fill_blank","skill":"grammar","prompt_es":"Complete the sentence","payload":{"type":"fill_blank","sentence":"Ya te lo he ___: el sábado no puedo.","translation":"I’ve already told you: I can’t on Saturday.","options":["dicho","dijo","dice"]},"answer":"dicho","accepted_answers":[],"explanation_es":"The participle of decir is irregular: dicho. Dijo (he said) is the preterite and dice (he says) the present.","difficulty":1,"recommended_seconds":15,"words":["es-he-dicho"]},{"id":"es-b1-u01-l02-e04","lesson_id":"es-b1-u01-l02","position":4,"type":"fill_blank","skill":"grammar","prompt_es":"Complete the sentence","payload":{"type":"fill_blank","sentence":"Este año he ___ muchos viajes.","translation":"This year I have taken a lot of trips.","options":["hecho","hizo","hago"]},"answer":"hecho","accepted_answers":[],"explanation_es":"The participle of hacer is hecho. Hacer un viaje means to take a trip, so he hecho muchos viajes is “I have taken a lot of trips”.","difficulty":1,"recommended_seconds":15,"words":["es-he-hecho"]},{"id":"es-b1-u01-l02-e05","lesson_id":"es-b1-u01-l02","position":5,"type":"fill_blank","skill":"grammar","prompt_es":"Complete the sentence","payload":{"type":"fill_blank","sentence":"Mis padres ___ vuelto a Mérida.","translation":"My parents have gone back to Mérida.","options":["han","ha","hemos","has"]},"answer":"han","accepted_answers":[],"explanation_es":"Mis padres is ellos, so haber is han: han vuelto. The participle vuelto does not change.","difficulty":1,"recommended_seconds":15,"words":["es-he-vuelto"]},{"id":"es-b1-u01-l02-e06","lesson_id":"es-b1-u01-l02","position":6,"type":"listen_choice","skill":"listening","prompt_es":"Listen and choose what you hear","payload":{"type":"listen_choice","audioText":"Jamás he dicho eso.","options":["Jamás he dicho eso.","Jamás he hecho eso.","Ya he dicho eso.","Jamás dije eso."]},"answer":"Jamás he dicho eso.","accepted_answers":[],"explanation_es":null,"difficulty":1,"recommended_seconds":15,"words":["es-jamas","es-he-dicho"]},{"id":"es-b1-u01-l02-e07","lesson_id":"es-b1-u01-l02","position":7,"type":"context_choice","skill":"reading","prompt_es":"Read and answer","payload":{"type":"context_choice","stimulus":{"kind":"reading","id":"es.stim.b1-u1-lista","document":"post","setting":"Paola is turning thirty and writes a post about her life so far.","title":"Cosas que he hecho antes de los 30","from":"Paola","lines":["El sábado es mi cumpleaños: ¡30 años! Estas son algunas cosas que he hecho.","He vivido en tres ciudades: Monterrey, Puebla y Mérida.","He escrito un libro de recetas, ¡pero nadie lo compró!","He hecho un viaje sola por Colombia y fue increíble.","Jamás he dicho que no a una aventura.","Siempre he vuelto a Puebla, porque allá está mi familia.","Todavía no he aprendido a manejar. ¿Y ustedes? ¿Qué cosas han hecho?"]},"question":"What has Paola written?","options":["A recipe book that nobody bought","A travel guide to Colombia","A novel about Puebla","A blog about her family"]},"answer":"A recipe book that nobody bought","accepted_answers":[],"explanation_es":"Paola writes “He escrito un libro de recetas, ¡pero nadie lo compró!”.","difficulty":1,"recommended_seconds":50,"words":[]},{"id":"es-b1-u01-l02-e08","lesson_id":"es-b1-u01-l02","position":8,"type":"context_choice","skill":"reading","prompt_es":"Read and answer","payload":{"type":"context_choice","stimulus":{"kind":"reading","id":"es.stim.b1-u1-lista","document":"post","setting":"Paola is turning thirty and writes a post about her life so far.","title":"Cosas que he hecho antes de los 30","from":"Paola","lines":["El sábado es mi cumpleaños: ¡30 años! Estas son algunas cosas que he hecho.","He vivido en tres ciudades: Monterrey, Puebla y Mérida.","He escrito un libro de recetas, ¡pero nadie lo compró!","He hecho un viaje sola por Colombia y fue increíble.","Jamás he dicho que no a una aventura.","Siempre he vuelto a Puebla, porque allá está mi familia.","Todavía no he aprendido a manejar. ¿Y ustedes? ¿Qué cosas han hecho?"]},"question":"What does Paola say she has never done?","options":["Said no to an adventure","Travelled alone","Gone back to Puebla","Lived in Monterrey"]},"answer":"Said no to an adventure","accepted_answers":[],"explanation_es":"She writes “Jamás he dicho que no a una aventura”. Jamás is a strong never.","difficulty":1,"recommended_seconds":50,"words":[]},{"id":"es-b1-u01-l02-e09","lesson_id":"es-b1-u01-l02","position":9,"type":"translate_write","skill":"translation","prompt_es":"Translate into Spanish","payload":{"type":"translate_write","source":"I have never said that."},"answer":"Nunca he dicho eso.","accepted_