rir","es-anunciar","es-informar"]},{"id":"es-b1-u10-l02-e03","lesson_id":"es-b1-u10-l02","position":3,"type":"fill_blank","skill":"grammar","prompt_es":"Complete the sentence","payload":{"type":"fill_blank","sentence":"___ el pronóstico, mañana va a llover.","translation":"According to the forecast, it’s going to rain tomorrow.","options":["Según","Para","Sobre","Por"]},"answer":"Según","accepted_answers":[],"explanation_es":"To say where information comes from, use según: según el pronóstico. Para mí gives your own opinion, and sobre means “about”.","difficulty":1,"recommended_seconds":15,"words":["es-segun"]},{"id":"es-b1-u10-l02-e04","lesson_id":"es-b1-u10-l02","position":4,"type":"fill_blank","skill":"grammar","prompt_es":"Complete the sentence","payload":{"type":"fill_blank","sentence":"Ella es ___ y trabaja en la radio.","translation":"She’s a journalist and works on the radio.","options":["periodista","periódico","artículo","titular"]},"answer":"periodista","accepted_answers":[],"explanation_es":"El periodista / la periodista: words in -ista don’t change for women. After ser, professions go without un / una: es periodista.","difficulty":1,"recommended_seconds":15,"words":["es-el-periodista"]},{"id":"es-b1-u10-l02-e05","lesson_id":"es-b1-u10-l02","position":5,"type":"fill_blank","skill":"grammar","prompt_es":"Complete the sentence","payload":{"type":"fill_blank","sentence":"Ayer el gobierno ___ que no hay clases hoy.","translation":"Yesterday the government announced that there are no classes today.","options":["anunció","anunciar","ocurrió","informar"]},"answer":"anunció","accepted_answers":[],"explanation_es":"Ayer asks for the preterite: anunció (it announced). Ocurrió means “happened” and does not take que + a sentence.","difficulty":1,"recommended_seconds":15,"words":["es-anunciar","es-el-gobierno"]},{"id":"es-b1-u10-l02-e06","lesson_id":"es-b1-u10-l02","position":6,"type":"listen_choice","skill":"listening","prompt_es":"Listen and choose what you hear","